window.harnessChunks["rows-4df1e7962f2ee60e.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T02:37:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shreyasic77/agentyou\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentYou</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shreyasic77/agentyou/tree/a8289d95608aa974f2a51aba68af5374bfa71d01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a8289d95608aa974f2a51aba68af5374bfa71d01\">a8289d9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce useful personal-assistant outcomes by interpreting the user&#39;s request and local personal evidence into answers, insights, routine/habit guidance and proactive advice. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user intents, note content, habit/routine state, changing completion history, timing/overload conditions, model uncertainty and cross-domain requests that require several evidence sources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: classify the incoming conversational need, choose the relevant specialist path and interpret retrieved/computed evidence into the substantive response or advice returned to the user. - Decision owner: the model actors in the shipped AgentYou graph — the LLM router for intent selection and the selected specialist/synthesizer model for the substantive user-facing result. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph edges/state, direct SQLite/Chroma reads, user-model context projection, deterministic statistical/risk computations, Streamlit presentation and model-provider adapters. - Closure path: user request → model intent classification → specialist evidence retrieval/computation → model interpretation/synthesis → response returned through the Streamlit chat; scheduled paths separately push deterministic first-party nudges into the UI queue. - Boundary reachability: ui/app.py directly invokes agents.orchestrator.chat, and the compiled first-party graph invokes the router and specialist LLM calls in the standard run path without requiring an external orchestrator implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic retrieval/statistics constrain the evidence available, but they do not determine the model&#39;s final interpretation, cross-domain synthesis or user-facing advice. The scheduled deterministic nudge path is treated as support/parallel operation rather than the basis for agent ownership. - Evidence: agents/orchestrator.py, agents/memoryagent.py, agents/habitagent.py, agents/routineagent.py, agents/nudgeagent.py, ui/app.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the app does not implement a general iterative tool-calling loop in its LangGraph specialists; A is credited for autonomous reasoning over first-party observations and response selection, not for MCP tool autonomy that the frozen graph does not wire.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination path is established among distinct operational units at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for a concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among specialist operations, not merely routing, parallel execution or shared persistence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party coordination judgment was established that detects an inter-S1 disturbance, selects an attenuating response and feeds that response back into later specialist behavior. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph conditional routing, asyncio.gather for multi-domain requests, shared ARIAState, shared user-model/SQLite state and deterministic scheduler timing. - Closure path: no qualifying disturbance → coordination response → changed subsequent S1 behavior loop is implemented. Multi-domain execution gathers three results and synthesizes them, which is task composition rather than interference regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the LLM router chooses a specialist from user intent and the multi node combines outputs, but neither path is evidenced as mutual adjustment against an inter-S1 conflict. Parallel specialist reads/writes do not become S2 merely because they share state. - Evidence: agents/orchestrator.py, core/usermodel.py, specialist implementations. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the parallel multi-domain path can cause specialists to touch shared persistent/user-model state in the same overall request; the frozen source does not pair that potential interference with an S2-specific collision-resolution mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established at the reviewed AgentYou organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for current cross-operation resource/commitment/prioritization/accountability decisions on behalf of the whole rather than interpreting the orchestrator name as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor is evidenced as holding a whole-organization current view plus authority to rebalance resources, commitments, priorities or interventions across specialist operations. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the intent router, static graph topology, multi-agent synthesizer, nudge priority scores, preferred-hour checks, daily nudge budget and APScheduler triggers. - Closure path: these mechanisms choose a request path or enforce preconfigured timing/budget rules, but no whole-system current-control decision → operational intervention → changed current organization loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: router and synthesizer models own operational interpretation, not metasystemic regulation; deterministic scheduler/nudge limits enforce configured bounds and do not own a current-control decision. - Evidence: agents/orchestrator.py, agents/nudgeagent.py, core/scheduler.py, ui/app.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a future supervisor that can inspect all active specialist work and change shared commitments/resources would require reassessment; the frozen graph executes one routed branch or a fixed three-specialist fan-out.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit relation is supplied in the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect model interpretations, stale/incorrect personal state and weak recommendations were considered, but no first-party runtime path obtains materially different operational evidence and independently challenges ordinary reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying audit owner is established that forms an independent judgment and returns findings into subsequent control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source citation in MemoryAgent responses, deterministic statistics, nudge deduplication and repository unit tests improve correctness but do not constitute runtime complementary audit. - Closure path: no independent audit finding → current-control response → changed subsequent operation loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specialist self-interpretation and ordinary data checks stay inside the production path; tests are development-only and are excluded from runtime ownership. - Evidence: agents/memoryagent.py, agents/habitagent.py, agents/routineagent.py, tests/testphase2.py. - Basis: explicit absence after boundary review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: citing retrieved notes gives evidence-grounded S1 behavior, not the independent complementary access required for S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a first-party future-facing adaptation path that turns longitudinal user behavior and workload/energy mismatch into concrete routine-change options for later operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persistent routine skipping, changing completion patterns, overload relative to the user&#39;s observed capacity and behavioral patterns that make the current routine a poor fit for future use. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which routine adaptation to adopt — remove/replace a repeatedly skipped item, reschedule it, reduce deep-work load or preserve a newly successful arrangement — and cause that choice to alter later routine behavior. AgentYou generates the options, but the shipped app does not own/apply that final activation decision. - Decision owner: constructor path only. First-party adaptation analysis deterministically generates specific options; a downstream autonomous actor/application must still be composed to select and apply them to persistent routine capability. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: analyseskippatterns, detectoverload, suggestadaptations, weekly report generation/scheduling, persistent user-model statistics, and separate MCP routine/user-model mutation primitives. - Closure path: historical user behavior + current capacity model → explicit future routine-change options → weekly/on-demand recommendation → missing binding in the standard app that selects/applies the option to persistent routine state → later operation. The exposed adaptation and mutation surfaces make the path constructible, but the activation edge is incomplete. - Boundary reachability: the option-generation functions are shipped in agents/routineagent.py and are invoked by the standard scheduled weekly report/on-demand RoutineAgent path; the first-party MCP server separately exposes persistent routine creation and user-model update primitives. No development-only actor is borrowed for the constructor claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an LLM may explain and prioritize already-generated recommendations to the user, but no model in the standard distribution is wired with authority to apply those changes. Deterministic heuristics create the options and the developer/downstream composition must supply autonomous selection/activation, so the state remains C rather than A. - Evidence: agents/routineagent.py, core/scheduler.py, mcpserver/server.py, core/usermodel.py, DEMOGUIDE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: habit correlations/best-time persistence alone are not credited as S4. The positive mapping is narrowly the explicit future routine-adaptation path; the demo documentation itself notes that a genuine response-feedback learning loop for nudge timing remains future work. - External distinction: observed user completion/skip behavior and workload fit relative to the user&#39;s recorded energy/capacity pattern. - Future / prospective distinction: whether the current routine arrangement is likely to remain viable on later days/weeks given repeated skips, overload or changing completion performance. - Adaptation option generated: remove/replace, reschedule, reduce workload or reinforce an arrangement that recently improved. - Path back into current capability / S3: the standard app surfaces the option to the user, while first-party MCP primitives can create routine state/update the user model; the frozen source does not bind recommendation selection to a routine mutation. A downstream actor/application must compose that final return edge.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established for the AgentYou organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user name/goals/preferences, agent system prompts and model/provider configuration shape context and behavior, but the review found no identity/ultimate-policy issue with a legitimate decisive authority and returned organizational closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying runtime right to decide AgentYou&#39;s ultimate identity, ethos or policy was established. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static specialist/system prompts, usermodel.identity/goals, nudge preferences, model-provider settings and the MCP usermodelupdategoals primitive. - Closure path: configuration/profile values feed later prompts, but no identity/policy issue → ultimate-authority judgment → returned governance path is implemented at the assessed recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the conversation agent can discuss profile setup and the MCP server can update user goals, but a profile/configuration edit is not S5 by itself and the standard chat graph does not wire an identity-level decision/escalation path. - Evidence: agents/orchestrator.py, core/usermodel.py, mcpserver/server.py, core/config.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the user&#39;s legitimate authority over their personal data/goals is not disputed; the negative finding is only that the repository does not turn that authority into a first-party closed S5 organizational conversation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ca3ce43e6f865bbd4bc0fb1d8ea3980060810fbb/assessments/agentyou.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ca3ce43e6f865bbd4bc0fb1d8ea3980060810fbb\">ca3ce43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T19:10:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ApodexAI/AgentHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Apodex AgentHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ApodexAI/AgentHarness/tree/7cc9e3bfca12ec44930073f20ee3f0732a7950da\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7cc9e3bfca12ec44930073f20ee3f0732a7950da\">7cc9e3b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a research question into an evidence-informed answer through selected web and calculation actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete results, changing query evidence, failed or empty tool observations and question-specific reasoning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next search/fetch/code call and arguments or emit the final answer. - Decision owner: the model-backed reactsolver agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider client, tool execution, role permissions, bounded iteration/retries, compaction and observer interventions. - Closure path: tool result enters message history; subsequent model turn changes evidence acquisition or returns a final answer, copied to terminal output. - Boundary reachability: the shipped reactbase node directly calls runagentloop with registered tools and returns its output; no developer must invent an additional agent workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive research choices disappear if the agent is removed while dispatcher and limits remain; the runtime does not prescribe the sequence of evidence requests. - Evidence: loop response and tools; reachable composition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-supported ownership, not an observed live quality or reliability measurement; bounded retries/forced summarization can terminate or constrain the actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material inter-S1 coordination function established at the single-solver boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate/empty queries within one solver are local S1 disturbances, not interference between distinct S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific coordination choice identified. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: single-node DAG, task IDs, stale-run checks and observer rollback. - Closure path: local result/rollback returns to the same solver; not inter-unit mutual adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool plurality and concurrent benchmark trials do not supply a coordination actor. - Evidence: one-node graph; scheduler. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: extensible graph types do not establish an additional shipped coordinated organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-cell current-control function separate from S1 execution and preselected limits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: token/context/time exhaustion and task interruption are enforced mechanically. - Decisive decision or feedback right: budgets and role resources are selected in profiles/configuration; no in-boundary discretionary whole-system regulation established. - Decision owner: configuration author/operator for setup; no evidenced S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BudgetObserver, ResourceManager, scheduler wall-time checks, pause hook and status transitions. - Closure path: configured thresholds stop/warn the loop; no qualifying autonomous or parent current-control decision-return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can choose ordinary task actions within limits but does not own allocation of a whole organization&#39;s current resources. - Evidence: budget enforcement; role resources; pause bridge. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: APIs that could support future supervision do not establish function-specific constructor or P closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary corrective audit of the operational cell established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: trajectory visibility, malformed calls and benchmark answer scoring. - Decisive decision or feedback right: observers apply routine production rules; benchmark judges compute evaluation results outside solver control. - Decision owner: no in-boundary independent audit actor with corrective return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trajectory/SSE observers, normalizers, rollback, answer extraction and separate benchmark judges. - Closure path: production observers alter the same loop; evaluation/rejudge produces scores rather than an in-boundary corrective-control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent model-based scoring alone cannot donate S3 to the running solver. - Evidence: observer assembly; benchmark runner; judges. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this does not deny the usefulness of external evaluation; it limits ownership to the declared runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective adaptation of the solver&#39;s present capability established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external web information relevant to the assigned question. - Decisive decision or feedback right: research actions answer the current task; no evidenced adaptation-option decision returned to present capability. - Decision owner: S1 agent for current research; no S4 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web tools, context compaction, profiles and traces. - Closure path: evidence changes the answer, not the organization&#39;s future capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external research is the primary transformation here, not automatically metasystem intelligence. - Evidence: research node; profile loader; workflow compaction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the model&#39;s training or future operator use of benchmark results is outside the assessed solver.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task scope and tool/model constraints fixed by setup. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party identity-level matter→legitimate authority→returned governing decision path found. - Decision owner: developer/operator sets static purpose and configuration; no evidenced S5 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, role permissions, profiles, limits, project license. - Closure path: static constraints govern actions, without a function-specific runtime policy dispute and authoritative return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model task answers and configuration enforcement are not ultimate-policy decisions. - Evidence: prompts; permissions; README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: legitimate maintainers exist; their repository development does not itself close runtime S5 at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/68c957118ac6daeb0ba347efcbed628b05565310/assessments/ap/apodex-agentharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 68c957118ac6daeb0ba347efcbed628b05565310\">68c9571 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:55:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ditto-assistant/ditto-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ditto Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ditto-assistant/ditto-harness/tree/8785d38b4aa64f7167860132204ec60c9cfece1b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8785d38b4aa64f7167860132204ec60c9cfece1b\">8785d38 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: answer personalized requests through a multi-turn model/tool loop, including selected memory lookups and writes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing natural-language queries, partial/noisy memory, unknown external tool results and repeated/failed calls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured model chooses which advertised tool and arguments to invoke or whether to deliver final text; subsequent choices incorporate returned tool outputs. - Decision owner: model-backed agent::Loop cell running inside the first-party loop, with host-selected model and tool inventory. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool trait, memory search/fetch/save tools, loop limits, cost collection, EventHandler, optional persistence, repetition detector and provider adapters. - Closure path: request and prepared memory context → Model::next → model-selected tool(s) → handletoolcall execution/result → appended conversation → next model choice or final answer; optional memory save closes the turn. - Boundary reachability: shipped chat::Harness::run constructs a real agent loop and passes memory plus injected tools, while the CLI exposes a standard chat mode and providers; not just a test fixture or proposed integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic tool choice and answer synthesis are delegated to the configured model; memory retrieval, persistence, concurrency and repetition counters are deterministic/runtime support. - Evidence: agent loop and calls, chat façade, memory tools. - Basis: first-party frozen source, executable model/provider interfaces and shipped CLI mode. - Confidence: high structural, no paid model E2E executed in this review. - Caveats: host tools are arbitrary capabilities; neither their presence nor the provider selection establishes autonomous metasystem control.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no inter-S1 collision/negotiation regulator established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple model tool calls and concurrent dream extractions could overlap in data, but no separate operational S1 actors with competing goals are coordinated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none over two independent operating cells. - Decision owner: none at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: async tool dispatch, SQL upsert conflicts, duplicate subject merge, per-run sequential model turns. - Closure path: tools return to one controlling model; no S1-to-S1 coordination contract changes two cells&#39; future actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel tools, SQL idempotence and a multi-call model response are one S1&#39;s procedures, not a multi-S1 coordination function. - Evidence: agent tool dispatch, dream merge. - Basis: frozen source, scoped negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an embedding application may run many loops and add coordination externally.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no regulator decides current resources/commitments for a whole population of S1 operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: request-level turn budgets, cost, repeated calls and memory retrieval limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: host chooses model, tools, max turns and memory options; Rust loop enforces bounds without discretionary portfolio decisions. - Decision owner: host configuration and deterministic loop rules; no first-party S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: max turns, cost collector, stopreason metadata, event callbacks, per-request retrieval configuration. - Closure path: requests terminate or emit progress; no whole-current task/agent commitment decision is fed into a distinct collection of S1 actors. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a per-run safety stop and billing data are not whole-system current regulation. - Evidence: RunRequest and Loop, chat request, README boundary. - Basis: frozen source, scoped negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the production Ditto backend and mining network can have other control systems, excluded from this repository boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent reviewer inspects raw task reality and feeds a veto/correction into organizational control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repetitive tool effects, incorrect answers or bad retrieved memory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detector reports repeated identical calls, but default event handler explicitly executes; optional host-supplied handler may choose Block, without first-party evidence audit. - Decision owner: host&#39;s injected handler for any repeat decision; no first-party complementary auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: repeat key/turn detection, tool progress/result events, optional future benchmark validator outside repo. - Closure path: detection may be passed to host before a repeated call; the shipped default performs it, and no independent verification finding is returned to a controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: matching successive tool call arguments is same-loop safety telemetry, not independently observing whether prior effects succeeded or whether claims match reality. - Evidence: EventHandler default, loop detector, README boundaries. - Basis: frozen source, scoped negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host may build external verification, but it must supply independent evidence and a corrective return before receiving S3 credit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external/future-facing adaptation analysis closes a return into current operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: historical personal memories, changing recalled subjects and retrieval ranking quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: offline Dreamer uses an LLM to extract/condense past subject facts; caller controls when it runs and the retrieval mode. - Decision owner: single-memory maintenance procedure and host, not a prospective environmental intelligence owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subject extraction, embedding dedup, MLP retrieval weights, optional reranker and vector database. - Closure path: historic memories become subject graph entries and improve later retrieval; no future external scenario or adaptation option is generated for changing the agent&#39;s capability/policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation and learned ranking are useful S1 information services, not S4 just because they persist over time. - Evidence: dream pipeline, retrieval, README. - Basis: frozen source, scoped negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications might add prospective strategy to this memory service, but none is first-party here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision loop for the harness installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider selection, tool availability, privacy scoping and storage of personal data rather than competing organizational ultimate purposes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: application developer/operator chooses tools, model, user scope and runtime bounds before a run. - Decision owner: external host; not first-party identity authority implemented in Ditto Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: userid/kgid/sessionid isolation, Options, host-injected tool trait, optional memory persistence. - Closure path: fixed host inputs constrain a run; no identity conflict rises to a legitimate authority that returns a policy decision into future agent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: normal configuration and data namespaces are not a standing policy-governance loop, and no closed-source product governance is borrowed. - Evidence: chat Options, agent Options, README scope. - Basis: frozen source, scoped negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: host applications may supply user-authentication, billing or institutional governance outside the assessed library.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0f8c15bd89c19253266b8438d7ff5e747ceb0f2f/assessments/di/ditto-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0f8c15bd89c19253266b8438d7ff5e747ceb0f2f\">0f8c15b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T15:46:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RapierCraftStudios/ForgeDock\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ForgeDock</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RapierCraftStudios/ForgeDock/tree/f5bfeeedf6baadd94aea1e6769f69abd45080016\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f5bfeeedf6baadd94aea1e6769f69abd45080016\">f5bfeee &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous model-guided GitHub issue software-engineering operations from investigate through implement, test and review to a bounded PR/closure receipt. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Variable codebase/issue text, unexpected tool errors, merge conflicts, failed CI, incomplete requirements, unexpected changes by other contributors and stopped/restarted agent execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured Claude Code/OpenCode agent or the shipped first-party headless Anthropic tool-use runner chooses code investigation, execution, file edits and review findings. runCommand calls a live model with tool schemas and acts on model-selected tooluse blocks, returns concrete tool results to the next model step, and limits iterations. runIssue/phase protocol advances based on GitHub/commit outcomes not arbitrary model success prose. - Decision owner: Model-driven execution agent chooses current investigation/build/review actions within first-party ForgeDock spec and tool handlers. Deterministic engine/projector and GitHub CI/merge evidence constrain acceptance and recovery; human can invoke or withhold credentials but is not each tool action&#39;s author. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Packaged commands/work-on.md and phase specs, bin/runner.mjs API/CLI backend selection, first-party tool handlers and output feedback, bin/engine.mjs phases/runlog/projector, GitHub FORGE markers, staged PR/CI evidence and lease recovery. - Closure path: GitHub issue and configured code workspace → model reads phase spec, inspects files and proposes/executes tools → tool outputs returned in iterative messages → model edits/tests/reviews → first-party engine observes git/CI/GitHub status and records durable FORGE annotations → next phase or needs-human/engine-error/merged terminal result. Reconciliation resumes after crashes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The distribution includes forgedock run-issue, CLI native Claude/OpenCode integration and a standalone model API runCommand with real tooluse/toolresult loop. A concrete configured provider and GitHub CLI/auth is needed but does not require downstream authors to implement a new agent actor. - Evidence: Without the configured model the engine can only route named phases and check evidence; it cannot select code changes, investigations or fix strategies. The meaningful task decision is agent-owned, rather than static shell-only orchestration. - Basis: bin/runner.mjs; bin/engine.mjs; bin/engine-cli.mjs; bin/engine/phases.mjs; commands/work-on.md; README.md. - Confidence: Primary runnable first-party source, model/tool handoff and shipped command specifications; no private upstream PR results credited as measurements. - Caveats: High for S1 agent action ownership with supported configured model. - undefined: Executing repo shell, branch or merge actions on a real target requires operator permission and can have significant side effects. This Index review ran no live model or target GitHub issue; README&#39;s claimed self-production metrics not independently reproduced. - Boundary reachability: bin/runner.mjs; commands/work-on.md; bin/engine.mjs: normal configured headless CLI/API route and packaged issue phases jointly provide a concrete agent actor, available tools, true task-variation feedback, on-disk/git observations and bounded terminal execution. The first-party runner does not require external agent authoring.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate autonomously owned regulator of interference between multiple independently viable continuing S1 business units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel issue lanes can touch the same files, exhaust worker capacity, collide on branch names or depend on each other&#39;s PRs; new findings can amplify work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ForgeDock resolves issue dependencies and same-path/overlap conflicts, sets concurrency and issue admission/defer rules, and can resume/stop lanes. Those are finite issue pipeline scheduling/resource constraints; no separate viable S1 enterprise units receive dynamically negotiated reciprocal operating commitments from an independent S2 owner. - Decision owner: Fixed implementation of scheduler/admission, configured forge.yaml bounds and operator&#39;s selected issue set; model lanes each own their current issue, not S2 balancing a set of organizational S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orchestrate DAG, lane sandbox/leases, phase completion predicates, cascade admission policy, conflict-aware overlap rules, issue labels and GitHub source of truth. - Closure path: Competing issue paths or worker exhaustion → static queue/defer/lease decisions → the affected individual issues launch serially or defer. No continuing interdepartmental disturbance feedback / negotiated commitments / return to viable units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concurrent coding issue pipelines are transient task instances, not necessarily viable operational departments. Merge-conflict avoidance does not automatically satisfy Beer S2 even if it dampens task collisions. - Evidence: commands/orchestrate.md; bin/engine/admission.mjs; bin/lean/execution.mjs; bin/engine/phases.mjs; forge.yaml.example. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting standalone/parallel and optional execution modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: If installed in a real multi-team company, it might help a human S2 coordinator, but team organization/authority would be external to shipped runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately authorized discretionary organizational S3 overseeing ongoing viable S1 unit objectives, allocations, commitments and management accountability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pipeline failures, stale sessions, pending PR reviews, test budget overruns, high-severity findings and resource contention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The deterministic phase engine/reconciler chooses next phase from observed markers and evidence; admission and max-concurrent rules are static, and human can configure concurrency, reject/merge PR or handle needs-human. These do not close a first-party parent-managed S3 over continuing independently viable S1 units. - Decision owner: Per-issue model agent and deterministic engine; human maintainer decisions on PR and project configuration, but not a published S3 parent decision/return over a viable departmental system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lease, crash recovery, phase verification, issue/PR status, work-on safety, resource caps, manual handoff and static config. - Closure path: Task result → status/phase updates → next issue action, or needs-human escalation → maintainer resolves one issue. No cross-unit whole-current authoritative manager report → dynamic resource/commitment choice for viable operating units → changed continuing S1 business performance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multi-issue queue supervision and human PR approvals are work control, not organizational S3. In particular the engine explicitly says it, not an LLM, picks phases deterministically. - Evidence: bin/engine.mjs; bin/engine/phases.mjs; bin/engine/reconcile.mjs; bin/engine-cli.mjs; commands/orchestrate.md; commands/work-on.md. - Basis: Source-bounded negative after checking autonomous and potential parent-governed pathways. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Specific owner approval steps might satisfy another organizational boundary if its viability and parent contract were separately supplied.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of S3 current-management claims from field reality and return of binding correction to S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model-generated code may be wrong, review may miss vulnerabilities, merged PR may regress CI, command output may exaggerate success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Domain-specialist reviewers can generate PR-specific findings, GitHub CI and engine detect merge/verification outcomes independently of model prose; these inspect software artifacts and run correctness, not an organizational S3 management statement about continuing viable units. - Decision owner: Agent review role and CI evidence guards; not independently chartered management-reality auditor with a separate operating-information mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Structured PR review comments, first-party review phase, issue/PR merge markers, GitHub evidence projector and status parity checks. - Closure path: Build PR → independent agents review code/test/CI → findings return to the issue and builder for remediation; no S3&#39;s whole-current state report → independent organizational field audit → S3 correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Good code QA is not the complementary organizational S3 audit relationship. The decision subject and return recipient are one coding deliverable and task worker. - Evidence: commands/review-pr.md; commands/work-on.md; bin/engine/phases.mjs; bin/engine/runlog.mjs; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative despite substantive independent work-product review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: GitHub reviewers may be humans; no independently authorized viable-unit organizational audit structure is assumed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No separately evidenced prospective outside-and-then agent ownership of long-term organizational adaptation and accepted changes to future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New security findings, codebase architecture drift, rising issue backlog, new upstream dependencies and CI failure signals can require new work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Autopilot periodically scans/reconstructs work and optionally raises fixes; review can file issues and dependency/health checks suggest improvements. The first-party mechanism does not provide a standing agent with a future-oriented environmental strategy mandate that selects and legitimizes new organizational capabilities and returns that acceptance to continuing S3/S1 operating structure. - Decision owner: Configured coding agent chooses remediation within current issue; human owner sets forge.yaml feature and project roadmap. Deterministic autopilot scheduling/admission does not own institutional prospective strategic choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Autopilot scan/fix command, GitHub Actions cron, issue-derived review findings, staged issue backlog, phase pipeline health/quality gates and memory annotations. - Closure path: Observed regression/CI/stale issue → new issue/remediation PR → merge when evidence gates pass. This updates particular software artifacts but does not by itself establish an institutional future-adaptation portfolio decision and formal return into ongoing organizational authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Creating issues or modifying software code is not sufficient to prove autonomous S4: the future strategic choice and owning organizational authority are not a distinct first-party closed function. A coding agent may answer strategic queries when prompted, but that is a bounded S1 task. - Evidence: commands/autopilot.md; templates/github-actions/forgedock-autopilot.yml; commands/orchestrate.md; forge.yaml.example; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking self-produced findings and regular unattended scanning, not just simple cron. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Long-running autonomous bug discovery may improve product resilience, but no general organizational adaptation-success claim follows.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No constitutional/ultimate identity or purpose authority, autonomous or operationally closed parent mode, for a viable organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Actions may violate target project&#39;s permission boundaries, GitHub auth may be revoked, secrets or unsafe tool capabilities can be misused, owner may change repository priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Static forge.yaml safety bounds, command specifications and project maintainer decisions control coding scope/merge. They do not form an identity-level institutional legitimate authority deciding the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose and returning a binding charter change to S3/S4/S1. - Decision owner: External repository owner and operator define purpose and rights; ForgeDock engine enforces local config and GitHub permissions. No first-party agent owns an ultimate organizational charter decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Local config permissions, host/provider credential authorization, GitHub branch protection, issue/PR review safety, human handoff. - Closure path: User config or GitHub protection → task allowed/blocked/needs-human; no purpose/identity conflict → constitutional decision by legitimate S5 → persistent institution-wide mandate returned to operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission enforcement, PR merge rights and static project objectives are supporting constraints, not a self-governing organization policy loop. - Evidence: forge.yaml.example; commands/work-on.md; bin/engine.mjs; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting native/human approval and auto-merge modes. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A deployed company may embed ForgeDock under its human S5, but that external governance is not a shipped ForgeDock decision-and-return mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/202fcc35ebdcb28bd85b77d1e626e4518739a355/assessments/fo/forgedock.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 202fcc35ebdcb28bd85b77d1e626e4518739a355\">202fcc3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:08:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ffffff233/DeepSeekFathom\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DeepSeekFathom</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ffffff233/DeepSeekFathom/tree/2c68eff7bb7ae2eabf4d36a0331386f06af2d356\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2c68eff7bb7ae2eabf4d36a0331386f06af2d356\">2c68eff &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute uncertain coding tasks through model-selected file/shell/patch/search tools while reacting to actual outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code structure, tool failures, unavailable files, incomplete edits, provider output and intermediate execution results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model selects the next tool, whether to delegate, repair after an error, or finalize in response to returned observations. - Decision owner: native model-directed FathomAgent together with the first-party tool-action/feedback loop; inference provider does not own the app&#39;s tool selection control wiring. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, turn/session records, context compaction, approvals, thinking/todo mode, hooks and result normalization. - Closure path: initial user task → model tool decision → first-party tool or ephemeral child executes → actual result appended to session → next model decision or final answer. - Boundary reachability: standard installed CLI/desktop constructs FathomAgent with its local registered tools; this loop is the shipped entry-point, not a benchmark fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s context-sensitive selection, tools, user approvals, session storage and a deterministic parser cannot decide situational coding actions. - Evidence: agent.py; cli.py; tools.py; tests. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for source reachability; provider-backed execution not reproduced independently. - Caveats: approved actions are bounded by real permission modes and the operator&#39;s configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party regulatory relation for specific interference or oscillation between multiple independent S1 operations was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegated tasks may address different subtasks, but current implementation returns each child&#39;s final summary in a parent-managed sequence with no shared conflict-feedback response. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model picks delegates and task descriptions, runtime creates bounded children. No autonomous S2 conflict intervention or returned peer agreement. - Decision owner: coding parent S1 for delegation; code for sequencing. No distinct coordination owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child specs, bounded permission modes, sequential child invocation and tagged event/summary handling. - Closure path: child outcome → parent task transcript; no detected inter-S1 interference → attenuating correction → subsequent changed peer behavior loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task division, routing and ordered execution do not suffice for S2 under Methodology 0.3.7. - Evidence: delegate agent implementation; README; tool contracts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user-supplied extensions may add coordination, but that configured external logic is not attributed to the first-party shipped mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-current authority owns portfolio priorities, current commitments, resourcing or accountabilities across operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task subtasks, todo items and permission modes are local present operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model manages its own coding approach; external user approves gated actions, chooses mode, and stops sessions. No whole-system managerial decision is returned to a set of sustained S1 units. - Decision owner: coding operative and human operator, not S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task todos, child delegation, process tool/status, permission constraints, storage and hooks. - Closure path: tool/todo/child result to same coding model, not separate whole-current status → S3 discretionary intervention → changed worker commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent planning its current work is a function within S1; it does not become S3 because it spawns helpers. - Evidence: agent loop; policy; CLI. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: local .air development scheduler records are explicitly outside packaged runtime ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary auditor with sufficiently independent evidence access, judgment and returned corrective path to S3 is wired into normal coding execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model-generated changes can be reviewed with diff/snapshot tools, optional delegated reviewer instructions or external hooks, but this is not an independent native whole-system audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operating model can inspect diff and decide whether to correct; user/developer may inspect history. No built-in separate auditor owns the audit verdict and current-control return. - Decision owner: coding operative for optional review, developer for external CI/hook behavior, not a credited S3 agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewStore, git snapshot/diff, delegated child text, hook event callbacks and tests. - Closure path: diff/read/test output → operator or same model coding loop; no independent direct-evidence audit judgment → managerial owner → binding correction to ongoing workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: review is a permission posture; .air/local/evidence/T2-review.md is development-only; a diff tool is evidence plumbing rather than independent audit closure. - Evidence: review service; agent delegation; hooks; review tests; README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported standard CLI. - Caveats: custom independently commissioned auditors could be composed externally, but are not first-party default ownership evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no integrated outward/future strategic adaptation loop changes the continuing agent organization&#39;s capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider aliases, current web search, skill inventory, plugins and updater are execution and human-managed configuration surfaces. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user selects model, permission mode, plugins/skills and optional update; the coding agent reacts to current task, not a future environmental-coupled capability portfolio. - Decision owner: human user/maintainer for product upgrades and configuration, present-task model for tactics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider registry, SkillStore, extension hooks, update checker and project instructions. - Closure path: external package/version observation or user option → manual product/config choice; no built-in future intelligence appraisal decision returning into whole-current organizational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: proactive current task steps, ordinary skill loading or package update are not automatically S4. - Evidence: updater; extensions; skills; agent. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: explicit human/parent adaptation may exist in the external development org but is not a first-party session-governed S4 parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/constitutional policy decision path for the agent organization is present in the shipped coding runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dangerous commands, write permissions, plugin trust and model identity instructions are tactical execution policy rather than ultimate organizational self-definition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses modes and confirmation, hook/config enforces declared constraints; no runtime S5 adjudicator decides continuing purpose/ultimate tradeoffs. - Decision owner: human for tool/trust config; deterministic policy checks for enforcement, without native S5 ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ApprovalPolicy, capability gates, HookRunner, provider identity guard and user-owned extension settings. - Closure path: configured mode/approval → permitted/blocked tool, not identity issue → legitimate parent/agent authority → binding whole-system policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: product identity prompt and permission modes don&#39;t confer ultimate organizational identity decision rights. - Evidence: policy; identity guard and tool loop; hooks; CLI config. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external humans maintain the software but their OSS governance is not operationally wired as session-level S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1a93f95e43fdf851192453f1c8e62b982b318d8a/assessments/deepseekfathom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1a93f95e43fdf851192453f1c8e62b982b318d8a\">1a93f95 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-21T17:40:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maref-org/maref\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MAREF (Multi-Agent Recursive Evolution Framework)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-21\">2026-10-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maref-org/maref/tree/35b1d8108887bf162711c2212b7a67d6e21d9342\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 35b1d8108887bf162711c2212b7a67d6e21d9342\">35b1d81 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A configured model-backed first-party recursive code-change work cell produces substantive source changes and evaluates their acceptance for a developer-defined system evolution task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Nontrivial architectural refactoring, code quality regressions, test coverage and generated source correctness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the normal configured OpenAI/Anthropic route, the model chooses task-semantic implementation text in response to a locally generated architecture proposal; the harness evaluates, deploys/rolls back and can observe another round. - Decision owner: LLMCodeGenerator with OpenAIProvider/AnthropicProvider supplies first-party invoked semantic code generation; the REL/SelfExecutor controls step admission and environment effects. External model compute does not own the harness architecture. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RecursiveEvolutionLoop OBSERVE→DIAGNOSE→ARCHITECT→CODEGEN→SAFETY→DEPLOY→VERIFY→EVALUATE; SelfObserver, SelfArchitect, LLMCodeGenerator, SelfExecutor, AST, tests, rollback and constitutional red-line guard. - Closure path: Local code and test observations become bounded architecture proposals; a model call generates change content; source is screened and deployed; verification feeds acceptance / rollback and further attempts. This is an operational loop with tool/file effects, not a static LLM API wrapper. - Boundary reachability: RecursiveEvolutionLoop.runsession() calls first-party observer/diagnostician/architect, then LLMCodeGenerator.generate, then SelfExecutor.deploy, then test/verification. The provider uses actual async OpenAI/Anthropic APIs when configured; absent keys, the fallback mock does not support a live cognitive claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configured model-generated source changes are first-party invoked and assessed within a bounded work-cell execution path; model inference alone does not carry ownership. - Evidence: src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py; src/maref/recursive/selfobserver.py; src/maref/recursive/selfdiagnostician.py; src/maref/recursive/selfarchitect.py; src/maref/recursive/llmcodegenerator.py; src/maref/recursive/selfexecutor.py; src/maref/evolution/constitutionharness.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuate reciprocal interference between multiple continuing, viable first-party agentic S1 units and return corrections to those units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent worker changes and conflicting ownership over shared work might require coordination. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A continuing coordinator would decide shared contention and return a changed action/commitment to distinct agent-owned S1 operations. - Decision owner: MultiAgentCoordinator dispatches caller-supplied BaseHarness objects and applies deterministic trust gates; no owner of reciprocal independent S1 operations is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sequential/parallel task dispatch with trust check, optional circuit breaker, logging/metering and result aggregation; proposed role labels. - Closure path: runall fans one task out to registered harnesses and gathers results. It does not show collision detection across continuing S1 operations or a resolution that returns to those agents. Parallel implementation holds a lock around the whole dispatch, in fact serializing the supposed parallel work. - Boundary reachability: Do not credit S2 merely from multiagent naming, adapter integration or preflight safety gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/execution/multiagent/coordinator.py; src/maref/execution/harness/unified.py; src/maref/loop/bridge.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Regulate current whole-system resources, operational commitments and exceptions across multiple viable operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cost/safety interruptions, agent failures, task timeouts and backlog would need system-level managerial decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose/revise global priorities or resource commitments, assign authority and return the decision to ongoing S1 units. - Decision owner: Deterministic FSM and circuit breakers enforce pre-supplied bounds on individual runtime/task loops; no S3 steward/controller with whole-system decision feedback established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gray-code governance FSM, GovernedLoop, per-execution circuit breaker, identity, tool permission, safety/quality gates and per-task cost caps. - Closure path: Stages advance along predefined local paths, or stop when preflight, cost, safety or test conditions fail. This is current-task execution safety, not an evidenced whole-of-organization management relation across ongoing S1 units. - Boundary reachability: Governance code is real and executable, but an implementation of constraints is not itself an S3 decision owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/governance/statemachine.py; src/maref/loop/bridge.py; src/maref/execution/harness/unified.py; src/maref/execution/multiagent/coordinator.py; src/maref/codegen/loop.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Complement routine managerial reports with an independent audit of operational reality and deliver discrepancies to an S3 management owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A first-party agent&#39;s own completion/health claims might be inaccurate or misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently inspect across reporting lines, judge discrepancies, and return findings to current organizational management for corrective intervention. - Decision owner: First-party AST/test/quality checkers, HMAC audit logs and verification registries primarily attest task/work-product facts; no independent complementary S3 reviewer and S3 feedback owner are evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Audit hash chains, snapshot/provenance and verifier consensus, source lint/tests, separate ratchet configuration guard. - Closure path: A failed code check blocks/rolls back the same code-change pipeline; its result does not challenge an S3 manager&#39;s global report through a distinct organizational channel. - Boundary reachability: No material S3 relation established; source verifies code/safety and records transitions, but this does not prove audit of an S3 account. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py; src/maref/recursive/selfexecutor.py; src/maref/execution/harness/unified.py; src/maref/evolution/constitutionharness.py; src/maref/governance/statemachine.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Construct prospective changes to the harness&#39;s own future coding/verification capability using observed system health, architecture and proposed source evolution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing module coupling, code coverage, recurring test failures and new technology constraints on future runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select an adaptation candidate targeting future capabilities and route it through bounded safety/deploy/verify; no separate independent S4 strategy authority is demonstrated. - Decision owner: The SelfArchitect and REL pipeline form first-party prospective options; the model may generate code for a candidate, but constitution/safety and transaction guards bound effect and do not constitute an independent strategic S4 regulator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SelfObserver.observecodebase/test coverage, SelfDiagnostician, SelfArchitect.proposeall, code generation, transactional deploy, tests, divergence detector and rollback. - Closure path: Observations about the system&#39;s own code/test environment generate future codebase improvement options and can lead to deployed capability modifications after checks; a subsequent observer could evaluate the changed codebase. Unlike mere per-task repair, this path targets the framework&#39;s future reusable code and tests. - Boundary reachability: RecursiveEvolutionLoop.runsession wires OBSERVE→DIAGNOSE→ARCHITECT→CODEGEN→DEPLOY→VERIFY→EVALUATE on the actual source tree. This supports a constructor-level prospective adaptation capability, not established stable autonomous S4 organizational governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The default runtime implements a concrete reusable prospective adaptation constructor but not independently owned strategic S4 authority. - Evidence: src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py; src/maref/recursive/selfobserver.py; src/maref/recursive/selfdiagnostician.py; src/maref/recursive/selfarchitect.py; src/maref/recursive/llmcodegenerator.py; src/maref/recursive/selfexecutor.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: moderate - Caveats: A test/coverage driven self-modifier is narrower than environmental market/strategy forecasting. It does not prove independent future-option appraisal or safe live deployment; S4=A would be unsupported.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimately arbitrate ultimate identity/purpose and constitutional policy for the agent organization and return it as operative authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts about permitted autonomous modification, safety red lines, final system identity and legitimate governance changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A legitimate S5 decision owner must adopt/revise ultimate policy and make an operative authority change that governs future S1 behavior. - Decision owner: A ConstitutionHarness compares strings and actor labels against fixed protected paths; there is no authenticated first-party organizational constitutional decision owner or policy-change transaction in the assessed runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ConstitutionHarness, freeze zones, safety state machine, rule/policy defaults and immutable-file restrictions. - Closure path: checkchange returns allowed/blocked for source modifications based on hard-coded rule lists, with actor=&#39;humanconstitutionmaker&#39; exempting selected red-line tests; neither verified human authority nor ultimate system-level policy adoption and return is shown. - Boundary reachability: Literal human-role string, governance slogans and static red-lines cannot be reclassified as a legitimate parent S5 function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/evolution/constitutionharness.py; src/maref/governance/statemachine.py; README.md; src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/926c22a06b83689fdd1f331894a7e2aa6dff84f7/assessments/ma/maref.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 926c22a06b83689fdd1f331894a7e2aa6dff84f7\">926c22a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T01:01:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onipounds/agent-loop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-loop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onipounds/agent-loop/tree/49525dc12a9861f71c99050de3b4a4b8b897e9fa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 49525dc12a9861f71c99050de3b4a4b8b897e9fa\">49525dc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform model-guided goal-facing telemetry/calculation queries through a first-party multi-turn tool/result/answer feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended user wording within model capability, different telemetry results, requested intermediate computations, tool errors, numerical hallucination and finite step budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured external model, reached through first-party Model.respond and runtime.run, chooses next tool names/arguments or final text after seeing tool outcomes and verification rejection feedback. - Decision owner: The model behind AnthropicModel owns the substantive per-task next-action discretion in the supported real-mode composition; user owns the top-level task and allowed tool environment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, JSON input schemas, exception capture, in-memory transcript, numeric grounding/recomputation, maxsteps and CLI error status. - Closure path: User goal → model choice → first-party tool execution → tool results in Memory → model&#39;s next decision; when final text appears, verify feedback either accepts the outcome or returns a correction signal to the same task loop. - Boundary reachability: CLI real mode instantiates AnthropicModel and calls the actual runtime.run. The Model protocol and tool-result writes are concretely connected in the shipped code, rather than merely documented as a future integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the real model leaves a bounded deterministic offline script and tool/verification infrastructure but no general adaptive model-guided task choice. The first-party harness implements the iterative operating feedback path, while the configured external model supplies the operational choice. - Evidence: agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/models.py; agentloop/cli.py; agentloop/tools.py; agentloop/verify.py; tests/testagent.py. - Basis: Explicit published model/tool integration plus structural wiring; external model success and reliability not empirically tested. - Confidence: High for this shipped control path; lower for performance of a live model/provider that was not executed. - Caveats: The verifier&#39;s &#39;verified&#39; flag is confined to its numeric heuristics and must not be generalized to total task validity; offline FakeModel is not independent proof of model autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately owned coordination function balancing mutual disturbances among two or more distinct viable S1 operating departments was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A run may contain several requested tools, but tool timing, input errors, task-step limits and result ordering remain local to the one S1 task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party S2-specific decision adjusting the relations/commitments of distinct S1 units; serial tool execution and shared transcript are mechanics. - Decision owner: One task model directs tool selection, and ToolRegistry executes; no distinct coordination owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry, step iteration, tool result list and task transcript. - Closure path: Individual tool outcomes return into the same S1 model context; no inter-S1 disturbance sensing → negotiation/attenuation → feedback into two separate operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An individual tool is not a viable S1 division and no S2-specific owner appears just because one model sequences calls. - Evidence: agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/tools.py; agentloop/memory.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative: first-party standard-distribution execution is one run with cooperating tools. - Confidence: High at this boundary. - Caveats: External orchestration could compose multiple independent instances and add S2, but this is not shipped by this repository.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current executive with a portfolio view and discretionary resource, obligation or priority control across operating S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Step exhaustion, incorrect task replies and failing tool calls change individual-run execution; they do not establish whole-organization allocation decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The CLI caller chooses maxsteps and the verifier deterministically rejects locally invalid answers; neither makes discretionary cross-S1 current-management decisions. - Decision owner: Host/user configures the task and step budget; task model handles its own loop; verifier enforces a limited predicate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxsteps termination, CLI exit code, checker callback, tool error handling. - Closure path: A candidate task answer → local verifier → retry or termination of that task. No distinct managerial current-state view, managerial decision, and returned commitments to independent S1 divisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The real run uses internal operational feedback, not a separate authorized executive that would survive removal of the model task owner. There is no operational parent-governed S3 return into a whole-system current-control decision. - Evidence: agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/verify.py; agentloop/cli.py; agentloop/evals.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after distinguishing run guards/QA from current organizational control. - Confidence: High for inspected distribution. - Caveats: Human choice of CLI flags, task and provider is external ordinary operator configuration, not evidence of S3=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently mandated complementary reality audit of ordinary reporting to a distinct S3 manager with returned corrective-control authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A numerical answer can misstate telemetry aggregates, and a test can observe an incorrect output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verify() checks the same in-loop answer against data already in that run&#39;s working transcript. It can reject and retry within S1, but does not independently audit S3 reporting via a complementary source. - Decision owner: The deterministic S1 verifier, invoked synchronously from runtime.run, has no separate auditor mandate/organizational owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Numeric grounding, raw-list recomputation, tests, offline eval and in-memory tool-results log. - Closure path: Tool outputs → same-run verification → model rejection/retry. Not a complementary operational reality path into an independent S3 executive and subsequent management correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even though verification is separate Python code, it acts as the task&#39;s immediate acceptance predicate. Removing a hypothetical complementary auditor changes nothing: no separate S3 or audit-return mandate is wired in. - Evidence: agentloop/verify.py; agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/evals.py; tests/testagent.py. - Basis: Source-specific bounded negative; does not conflate QA with S3. - Confidence: High at this assessed recursion. - Caveats: Third-party auditors could inspect tool logs outside the package; such a system is not included.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party function observes changing external environments to generate or commit prospective modifications to future operating capability or strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar future tasks or changing sensor conditions could motivate changed tools, models or evaluation coverage. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The task model reasons about the current question, while developer-provided tools, model selection and offline scripts remain fixed. Evals score current sessions; no identified option-selection and future capability update closes back into operation. - Decision owner: User/developer decides future software changes externally; the deployed loop uses the currently supplied tools and model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evaluations, optional model selection, scripted offline planner, README scaling roadmap and parameterized tools. - Closure path: Task → current answer/evaluation output; no outside intelligence → prospective adaptation option → authorized decision → changed subsequent tool/policy/runtime capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An external model&#39;s choice of the next task action is S1 operating feedback, not a distinct organizational future-adaptation right. An eval score is not evidence that the package rewrites its own capability. No implemented parent-governed S4 feedback return is shown. - Evidence: agentloop/evals.py; agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/models.py; agentloop/offline.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting runtime, evals, supported modes and future-roadmap distinction. - Confidence: Medium-high; code is small and modes are explicit. - Caveats: An integrator might manually use evaluations to develop a new version, outside the assessed first-party supported operating loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party constitutional authority that decides the system&#39;s identity, ultimate purpose or governing policy and returns a binding policy choice into subsequent operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task goals, allowed tools, provider credentials and model settings require authority boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The CLI user/developer supplies the goal/provider/configuration; ToolRegistry grants a fixed set of tools and runtime checks ordinary execution budgets. No higher-recursion policy matter reaches a first-party legitimate parent S5 decision-and-return loop. - Decision owner: External user/developer owns purpose and package design; runtime delegates task action choice to the model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI parameters, pyproject packaging, fixed tool schemas, MIT license, README governance and numeric task acceptance. - Closure path: Operator task and existing configured policies → one run; there is no explicit identity/policy issue → legitimate parent authoritative deliberation/decision → binding return changing future first-party system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool limits and a human-selected task do not give the agent or an implemented parent mode the ultimate identity/policy right. Repository maintainers own changes to source externally but are not a packaged parent decision loop for the assessed agent&#39;s organizational identity. - Evidence: agentloop/cli.py; agentloop/tools.py; agentloop/runtime.py; README.md; pyproject.toml. - Basis: Bounded negative for a single-run task harness and its supported CLI distribution. - Confidence: High for this scoped claim. - Caveats: Human project ownership/license is real but is not evidence of S5=P under this operational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/212837b40b31341b8e4493f347ac13e165bce60b/assessments/ag/agent-loop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 212837b40b31341b8e4493f347ac13e165bce60b\">212837b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:10:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Zleap-AI/Zleap-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zleap-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Zleap-AI/Zleap-Agent/tree/8aa5f07a20c535687ee39c6dccade7869ae27fad\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8aa5f07a20c535687ee39c6dccade7869ae27fad\">8aa5f07 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete user and scheduled work through model-selected specialized workspace tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: differing user tasks, workspace capabilities, tool errors, missing context and provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose workspace/action, interpret tool results and continue or conclude the assigned work. - Decision owner: Main and specialized workspace model agents within host-selected scopes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kernel/runtime tool invocation, scoped capability bindings, model adapters, turn limits, approvals, session persistence and compaction. - Closure path: user/task input → Main/workspace model → tool result → subsequent model turn → workspace result/artifact and user reply. - Boundary reachability: supported Web/CLI and task worker instantiate ConversationService/ChatEngine; the kernel invokes the registered workspace runtime. No external agent&#39;s vector is inherited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-specific next actions depend on model decisions, while deterministic machinery enforces preselected constraints. - Evidence: kernel dispatch, workspace tool loop, Main switch path, worker operation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: preview source availability; no empirical model reliability or viable-organization certification claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate stale-history interference between conversation runs and repeated/cyclic workspace handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing request/conversation agent runs and specialized workspace operations that can hand work onward. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent messages using one durable conversation could read stale history before either persists; workspace A → B → A handoffs or repeated completed dispatch can replay the same work. - Attenuating coordination relation: keyed serialization around load/run/persist, visited-workspace/depth guards and duplicate-dispatch feedback carrying the previous completed result instead of executing again. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the next lock holder loads history after the prior run; blocked handoffs become returned failure/switch results and duplicate dispatch returns prior status/carry-back into Main&#39;s next context. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source identifies the actual stale-read critical section and implements explicit re-entry/duplicate suppression. Generic workspace isolation or the switch API alone would not suffice. - Disturbance / variety regulated: interacting runs&#39; shared conversation state and workspace-chain oscillation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the serialized scope and cycle/replay suppression policy; autonomous revision of that coordination response remains to be composed. - Decision owner: developer-selected deterministic policy at this boundary; no autonomous agent coordination regulator is supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: KeyedMutex, visited sets, depth ceiling and projected failure/duplicate tool results. - Closure path: conflict candidate → fixed gate/lock → suppressed or delayed operation → returned state/current history → subsequent operational choice. - Boundary reachability: ConversationService owns the mutex; shared worker/gateway compositions reuse a service. ChatEngine&#39;s chain/replay protection is on the standard workspace-dispatch path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed mechanisms operate without agent discretion. They are function-specific first-party coordination paths supporting C, not evidence for A. - Evidence: identified critical section and lock, load/run/persist wiring, handoff-cycle suppression, duplicate result return, shared gateway service. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: mutex is per service/process, not a distributed shared-file lock. Web constructs a service per request, so no claim of cross-request Web locking is made. Serial delegation by itself is not the positive witness.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: supervise the operator-owned current scheduled-work portfolio and revise commitments, model/workspace allocation or execution constraints in response to current state. - Whole-system current view: task panel/API lists the owner&#39;s tasks and recent runs; detail views retrieve run history, status, summaries/errors and next/last run information. - Current-control decision scope: pause/resume/delete tasks, revise prompt/schedule, select model/project/workspace/permission mode, or request an immediate run. Scope is the visible owner&#39;s portfolio, not every tenant or hidden runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unwanted/repeated commitments, failed runs and unsuitable current task bindings or execution constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which current commitments/resources/constraints to change after reviewing the portfolio and its results. - Decision owner: human deployment owner in P mode; autonomous supervisory judgment must be composed for the C mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: authenticated actor/owner scope, task registry, schedule synchronization, pg-boss, worker task re-read and persisted run reports. Queue timeouts/reclaim are fixed enforcement, not autonomous S3. - Closure path: operator sees task/run state → authoritative task update → service persists and synchronizes schedule → worker reads current configuration/enabled state → subsequent operation and new run feedback change. - Boundary reachability: supported Web task pages call the task API and service; supported task worker consumes the same store/queue. Web chat separately wires the Main task-management tools to this service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no shipped autonomous observe-portfolio/decide-intervention cycle. Main task tools are explicitly user-request-driven; autonomous regulation is a constructor possibility through the already function-specific ledger/update path. - Evidence: operator panel, whole portfolio plus recent runs, scoped updates and schedule closure, worker enforces current task, constructor tool, Web tool wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: P is current-control over this operator&#39;s deployment commitments; not a generic high-risk-tool approval and not organization-level governance. Pausing changes future dispatch; immediate cancellation of all already-running jobs is not claimed.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary independent operational audit loop supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary execution errors, reported outcomes and context/memory quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separately owned independent audit judgment and corrective-return right established. - Decision owner: producing runtime/model and operator handle ordinary run feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run records, traces, artifact snapshots, sensitivity checks and repository tests. - Closure path: ordinary production status/QA, not a separate complementary audit-to-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit-named persistence or static checks do not provide an independent assessor with different access to operational reality. - Evidence: run audit projection, ordinary workspace processing, memory dream processing. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: arbitrary custom audit work can be added, but is not an evidenced shipped audit mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current requests, memory/context relevance and reusable operational lessons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no evidenced prospective adaptation-options decision linked to current capability regulation. - Decision owner: task agents handle current work; operator configures capabilities and models. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workspace research, skill discovery, memory recall/experience extraction, dream reconciliation and configuration UI. - Closure path: memories and skills re-enter future task context; this is not a distinct external/future adaptation conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retrospective experience processing and generic tool/model selection alone do not close S4. - Evidence: dream payload and memory writes, memory orchestrator, supported capabilities/entry points. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: externally informed research can be a user&#39;s ordinary task without constituting this deployment&#39;s S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime ultimate-policy/organizational identity resolution loop supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task permissions, persona facts and access to shared memory/capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy case routed to a legitimate ultimate authority with a specific returned governance decision. - Decision owner: host/operator chooses persona and access settings; model applies them and stores allowed descriptive memory. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: non-overridable soul rules, avatar/system configuration, approval modes and creator/admin restrictions on global self-memory. - Closure path: configuration/memory affects context and tool permission; no distinct ultimate-policy adjudication process established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persona text, agent nickname/self-facts and a high-risk-action approval are not identity-level regulatory closure. - Evidence: persona/rules distinction, self-memory scope/actor policy, runtime self-memory guidance, tool policy. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: legitimate operator authority exists, but only a function-specific closed S5 path would justify P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6c38673690cb1caa507b40ca23bc8e4fda8bb152/assessments/zl/zleap-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6c38673690cb1caa507b40ca23bc8e4fda8bb152\">6c38673 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T00:43:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jwangkun/Pi-Multi-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pi-Multi-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jwangkun/Pi-Multi-Agent/tree/d2fef0f30a762ec04eff9d8dde6cd406648955bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d2fef0f30a762ec04eff9d8dde6cd406648955bc\">d2fef0f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-backed work cells turn user goals and subtasks into generated outputs or tool-mediated action within the supported installed library modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable task text, model output, tool responses and failed attempts; an actor must interpret each returned result and choose subsequent content or tool calls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A model-backed AgentExecutor chooses task response; in the cluster mode, the model selects offered function calls or a final answer, conditioned by prior tool output. - Decision owner: The composed configured autonomous model-backed work-cell actor. First-party TypeScript executes transport/retry/tool effects; external model inference supplies bounded work discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Prompt construction, context, tool definitions, message bus, state events, timeouts/retries, concurrency and token budgets, model router, result persistence. - Closure path: Goal/subtask → first-party Agent.execute or AgentCluster.executeLLMCall → model response/tool-call choice → first-party tool execution and returned result → next model turn or task completion → output to user/plan. - Boundary reachability: Both Agent.execute() and cluster executeSubTaskWithTools() are shipped and exported or invoked from public APIs, not in sample/test code only. The latter loops over real model-reported tool calls and feeds tool returns into the next model request. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model-backed actor leaves deterministic calls and retries, but not the flexible task-content/tool-choice decision. The package&#39;s own agent loop operationally wires those decisions rather than merely naming an external agent. - Evidence: agent model executor and result; cluster model/tool loop; public exports. - Basis: Structural model-to-tool-to-model wiring; documented supported model-backed modes. - Confidence: High for first-party implementation path; no empirical assertion of task effectiveness. - Caveats: The generic AgentExecutor is injected by the embedding application; first-party AgentCluster supplies the concrete OpenAI-compatible tool-call loop. The external provider is not itself part of the package&#39;s codebase.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: In the exposed debate mode, mitigate incompatible contributions from two or more independently answering agent work cells by returning their earlier positions as a shared dispute context for later rounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Contradictory claims or counterarguments across S1 work cells can make the group&#39;s combined response incoherent. - Distinct S1 units: The separately configured LLMAgent actors passed into executeDebate, each with its own system prompt and model invocation. - Inter-S1 disturbance: The built-in debate procedure intentionally elicits differing positions and counterarguments; unresolved cross-agent disagreement is the structurally represented interference, not mere concurrent invocation. - Attenuating coordination relation: debateHistory accumulates each agent&#39;s previous position; subsequent round &gt; 0 prompts include those other arguments and request counterarguments/supporting evidence; an LLM moderator then summarizes a consensus candidate. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The history is included in each actor&#39;s next-round prompt, changing its available distinctions and potential reply; moderator synthesis provides a collective outcome to the caller. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This shipped method explicitly stages disagreement, re-presents opposing positions to the same actors and seeks a reconciled summary, unlike generic message-bus, dependency graph or speaker-selection APIs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Framing disagreement as counterargument on the next agent turn and proposing synthesis after the rounds; the first-party method supplies a particular reconciliation channel, but developer-supplied agents and prompts determine substantive convergence. - Decision owner: First-party S2-specific constructor for the debate feedback relation; the configured model-backed debate participants and synthesized moderator judgment own content choices. No general cross-team autonomous conflict policy is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Debate transcript accumulation, fixed maxRounds, serial model requests and final moderator request. - Closure path: Two or more agents provide positions → debateHistory collects responses → successive turns receive opposing positions → mediator model produces a consensus summary returned to the caller. - Boundary reachability: LLMAgentCollaboration.executeDebate is exported in the first-party public library API and directly implements the feedback path; it requires the integrating developer to supply at least two agent configurations and invoke the mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent replies to conflict are model-authored, but the coordination mode and final synthesis are intentionally constructed by the framework and do not give an autonomous controller discretion to configure the whole debate/conflict policy. Hence C, not general S2 A. - Evidence: LLM debate implementation; API export; contrast: shared memory. - Basis: Structural first-party debate-with-return path; inferred organizational disturbance as opposing work-cell claims. - Confidence: Medium. Code proves the feedback construction, not that every model debate reduces actual conflict. - Caveats: This state is limited to the opt-in debate mode, not generic inter-agent messaging. The separate DebateAndConsensus implementation in patterns.ts does not replay its accumulated history into future rounds, so it is not used as the S2 witness. No cross-run contention governance or demonstrated autonomous negotiation authority is claimed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Monitor the whole current deep-mode goal execution and regulate which weak work cells receive higher priority and revised instructions on the next iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subtask output failures, underlength responses and insufficient quality across an active goal plan threaten the entire deliverable under bounded retries/iterations. - Whole-system current view: AgentCluster maintains one currentPlan, results across subtasks, progress map, token usage and scoring over all plan subtasks, with access to DeepPlanner-supplied priorities. - Current-control decision scope: replanForIteration checks results against weak-task criteria, raises weak task priority to high and modifies their prompts; the modified plan is used for the next execution iteration and per-task model selection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select current weak tasks for increased priority and mandatory additional evidence/detail; presently implemented by a developer-coded threshold and template, not discretionary whole-system agent governance. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic constructor implementing a narrow quality/current-allocation relation. Operator sets maximum iterations, initial goal and provider; the model does not own the hard-coded weak-task threshold. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared memory, progress/events, model router, tool records, fixed maxIterations, score threshold, queue and timeout/budget settings. - Closure path: Complete an iteration → review all current subtask outputs/quality score → flag weak tasks and revise plan priorities/prompts → executeIteration / resolveComplexityHint consumes modified task attributes in the next run. - Boundary reachability: executePlan invokes evaluateIterationResults() and replanForIteration() directly, replaces currentPlan, then runs the next iteration; model selection reads the same updated SubTask.priority in this shipped deep mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The current-control adjustment is observable in shipped code, but removing a putative manager LLM leaves the same hard-coded weak-task threshold/priority decisions. Thus this is narrow S3 construction C, not A; runtime enforcement and budget limits have no independent managerial ownership. - Evidence: whole plan/quality/replan loop; current result scoring and intervention; revised task model selection. - Basis: Structural, confined to the deep goal-team recursion. - Confidence: Medium for function scope and constructor ownership; low for empirical quality effectiveness. - Caveats: This is one goal plan, not a live organization-wide resource manager. No adaptive autonomous cross-goal budgeting, conflict intervention or parent S3 mode is established. Orchestrator worker assignment, token-budget enforcement and dashboard status alone are not additional S3 evidence.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No first-party sufficiently independent complementary access to operational reality is established beyond ordinary production quality checks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Work cells might misstate facts or output correctness, but normal critic/quality scoring is embedded in the production loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate audit judgment with independently sourced operational facts and returned corrective S3 decision is evidenced. - Decision owner: Ordinary model critic/evaluator and deterministic quality heuristics support production QA, not an independent metasystem auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DeepEvaluator, heuristic iteration score, CriticReviewer, reviewer round, log and progress snapshots. - Closure path: Critic feedback can reach the creator in a routine production review; the final DeepEvaluator score affects the final success classification but does not initiate a complementary audit correction of a separate operational report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second model call or different prompt does not by itself establish independence of evidence, ownership or an alternative audit channel. - Evidence: DeepEvaluator input/output and fallbacks; producer-owned quality iteration; routine critic-reviewer. - Basis: Bounded negative first-party source inspection. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Embedding applications could separately implement external audits; their unshipped organization is outside the declared library boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supported first-party loop models a future external environment, generates prospective capability options and returns an adaptation choice into present operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model capability/provider changes, users and research-domain shifts could require adaptation, but present wiring only handles the current assigned goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established for outside-and-then adaptation; task replanning improves current responses under existing capabilities. - Decision owner: Human/developer chooses model/provider settings and upgrades; no evidenced agent S4 or function-specific parent adaptation mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model registry, complexity estimator, tool search, current-goal DeepPlanner, memory and current-iteration replan. - Closure path: The runtime reacts to current task and quality feedback; it does not propose an externally grounded future organizational adaptation and enact it through S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model routing chooses configured endpoints for current subtasks, while the planner changes the current deliverable—not the organization&#39;s future capability/operating model. - Evidence: task-centric deep planning; short-loop replanning; model registry/routing. - Basis: Bounded negative at the shipped runtime modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can ask the task agents to write a future-looking research report, which is an S1 task output, not automatically adaptation of this harness&#39;s operational capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return path is supplied for this multi-agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Role boundaries, sensitive tools, provider credentials and resource budgets create policy concerns, but shipped controls are static user/developer configuration and task execution gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established at identity/ultimate-policy level. Runtime validates workflow metadata/schema and observes execution constraints; operator config sets goals and credentials. - Decision owner: Human/developer provides overall purpose and limits externally; model-backed worker prompts do not receive ultimate identity authority. No packaged parent mode closes identity-level disputes and feeds binding governance back. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System prompts, TokenBudget, concurrency limit, tool list, prompt/schema validation, cancellation, config and runtime errors. - Closure path: Configured constraints restrict a run, but no identity/policy issue is routed to legitimate authority and its resolution returned into ongoing organizational policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The ability to generate or execute a script and enforce an operator&#39;s token budget is not the right to revise ultimate policy. - Evidence: workflow runtime constraint path; fixed agent config and tool selection; public modes. - Basis: Bounded negative source review. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The maintainer&#39;s outside repository governance and a private downstream organization&#39;s human policy are not credited as this installed harness&#39;s S5(P).\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d9c9d3487ace2ec2c2c5a05a90a8deedea63fffb/assessments/pi/pi-multi-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d9c9d3487ace2ec2c2c5a05a90a8deedea63fffb\">d9c9d34 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T19:11:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kju4q/q-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">q-agent-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kju4q/q-agent-harness/tree/37903fc742420f56e9568c6b45c9b89035a49139\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 37903fc742420f56e9568c6b45c9b89035a49139\">37903fc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Transform a topic into a finished draft with autonomous file-backed iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Draft defects, weak direction, format and factual/clarity problems. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the plan and content, judge defects, edit the draft and replan after repeated failure. - Decision owner: The agent enacting the shipped content-loop role in the explicitly supported host. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: External host inference/file tools; separate plan.md/draft.md state and iteration instructions. - Closure path: Agent creates plan and draft → inspects draft against criteria → edits/revises plan → repeats until done, replanning after five unsuccessful iterations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The host supplies machinery while the installed agent selects content and corrective actions. Markdown implementation does not by itself preclude agent ownership. Only this concrete supported role is credited, not all generic toolkit aspirations. - Evidence: content-agent-loop/README.md; content-agent-loop/loop.md; content-agent-loop/workflow.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Host-dependent prompt-level execution; no bundled inference runtime or live compliance/quality demonstration is claimed. - Boundary reachability: README supplies the installation trigger (copy loop.md into Claude Code/Cursor and give a topic); loop.md contains the complete role/action/feedback instructions, with no bespoke local workflow required.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No material inter-S1 coordination path in the supported content cell. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Content revisions are local to one acting agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Separate plan/draft files and phases are state separation, not distinct S1 units. A separate reviewer is an advanced customization, not an installed collision-regulation path. - Evidence: content-agent-loop/loop.md; content-agent-loop/workflow.md; README.md; VERIFICATION.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system current-control function beyond local work and operator approvals. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated draft failure and memory size need management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Five-iteration replanning and memory token bounds are local operational rules; operator approval of a memory promotion is not whole-current resource/commitment regulation. - Evidence: content-agent-loop/loop.md; scripts/compact; scripts/hotmem; COMPACTION.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary independent audit path in the installed role. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-critique can reproduce the writer&#39;s blind spots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The content agent reviews its own draft; the guide&#39;s external-review prompt is ordinary end-of-project acceptance, with a separate model left as advanced composition. - Evidence: content-agent-loop/loop.md; content-agent-loop/workflow.md; VERIFICATION.md; docs/harness-loop.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeating task lessons and stale memory affect later outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: compact and monthly review improve internal procedures from past task logs. The Harness² recipe replays an already failed task; describing outside research does not itself close a prospective environmental adaptation function. - Evidence: scripts/compact; COMPACTION.md; docs/harness-loop.md; content-agent-loop/workflow.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky operations and persistent memory rules constrain agent behaviour. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The example approval policy and per-proposal yes/no gate govern operational changes; generic hot-memory rules do not establish an identity-level issue and legitimate ultimate-authority return. - Evidence: scripts/compact; COMPACTION.md; docs/approval-policy.md; AGENTS.md; content-agent-loop/loop.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b817cfc3b7de3e5f1dad9e7306c2b894e67d86c9/assessments/q-/q-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b817cfc3b7de3e5f1dad9e7306c2b894e67d86c9\">b817cfc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T15:29:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/qujck/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/qujck/harness/tree/9a2fc16d1cc26070114ae6e908107cf211e0c99e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9a2fc16d1cc26070114ae6e908107cf211e0c99e\">9a2fc16 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement an assigned feature ticket to a reviewed terminal disposition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific source/test uncertainty and failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose investigation, edits and repairs within a ticket. - Decision owner: model-backed developer session under the shipped role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tmux/Claude launcher, identity environment, isolated worktree, ledger, verification and handoff scripts. - Closure path: spawn passes read-role/init/claim instructions and ticket; developer decides actions, consumes tests/review and completes/parks/releases the record. - Boundary reachability: team/agent-spawn.sh builds an actual claude command and first prompt, including the shipped developer role, rather than only describing an aspirational agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the implementation; launcher/gates enforce mechanics. Standard installation supplies commands/credentials, not a newly invented S1 actor policy. - Evidence: team/agent-spawn.sh; docs/roles/developer.md; AGENTS.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent duplicate ownership and shared-checkout corruption. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two developer ticket units claim the same ticket or mutate the same checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept one claim, refuse the competing claimant and return it to another ticket; isolate each writer. - Decision owner: developer/lead coordination policy must be composed with first-party conflict-response primitives. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic remote Git branch claim, ledger claim, identity/live-marker checks and per-agent worktrees. - Closure path: feature-ticket claim rejection explicitly tells the loser to choose another ticket; init refuses an occupied checkout and names isolation remedy. - Boundary reachability: the installed developer&#39;s prompt invokes claim/init before work; these are S2-specific paths in executable first-party scripts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C reflects supplied collision response with autonomous coordinating actors/communication still commissioned; neither a push lock nor a generic mailbox is an agent. - Evidence: scripts/feature-ticket.sh; scripts/init.sh; docs/roles/developer.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Source-relative ownership/reachability finding; no live model or effectiveness test. - Distinct S1 units: parallel developer sessions owning different or competing tickets. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate claim race and same-checkout corruption are explicitly documented and refused. - Attenuating coordination relation: single accepted remote lock/ledger holder and separate writer worktrees. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: losing claimant must select another ticket; occupied-checkout init refuses progress until isolation/legitimate handoff. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: claim refusal targets a concrete shared-work race and returns a changed work-selection condition.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the current team&#39;s queue, work allocation, team size and shared account commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: idle/blocked workers, ready-work demand, red main and current budget/capacity pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base constructor: commission a lead with queue/routing/spawn/bench authority; parent mode: owner authorizes off-budget/current-capacity exceptions or revises weekly plan. - Decision owner: base C: configured delivery-lead actor/communication/authority arrangement; P: legitimate local project owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ledger/frontier, team panel/context publisher, budget calculation, spawn/bench and owner-word records. - Closure path: lead instructions connect current record → routing/team changes; owner budget answer is returned verbatim or through approve/adjust, altering subsequent spawn acceptance. - Boundary reachability: lead role plus onboarding launch-line procedure is an intentional first-party constructor; owner-approved budget flags/adjustment scripts are actually consumed by spawning. Non-worker launch requires manual role commissioning; it is not graded autonomous A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: telemetry and hard cap do not choose staffing. A lead&#39;s missing commissioning prevents A, while the explicit local owner resource judgment supplies the distinct parent loop. - Evidence: docs/roles/delivery-lead.md; scripts/agent-onboard.sh; team/agents-budget.sh; team/agent-spawn.sh - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Source-relative ownership/reachability finding; no live model or effectiveness test. - Whole-system current view: lead reads team ledger claims/frontier, red-main state and cross-session context/account usage. - Current-control decision scope: shared staffing/model allocation, routing, budget exceptions and current work priorities within the owner&#39;s envelope.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge developer completion/correctness claims from original change evidence and return findings to delivery control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: developer report or green routine checks miss a substantive defect. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge defects separately from authorship and decide how findings affect held/reworked PRs. - Decision owner: a separately commissioned code-reviewer actor judges; delivery lead acts; neither is supplied as an active default session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate role/session/worktree, raw PR diff/code/tests, durable line comments and lead-directed messages. - Closure path: reviewer posts verified line findings before messaging lead; lead decides holds and routes repair; moved commits return for review. - Boundary reachability: code-reviewer.md and onboarding role/launch instructions expose the specific independent raw-artifact and corrective-routing path, but commissioning/host messaging/authority must still be composed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C is specific independent judgment/feedback construction, not an automatic grade for tests or labels. The separate role cannot write fixes, merge or take author direction; routine mandatory testing alone is not credited. - Evidence: docs/roles/code-reviewer.md; docs/roles/delivery-lead.md; scripts/agent-onboard.sh - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Source-relative ownership/reachability finding; no live model or effectiveness test. - Claim being audited: developer&#39;s completed PR implements ticket requirements without defects. - Ordinary reporting path: developer handover/test readings and CI to the lead. - Complementary access path: reviewer reads original ticket/diff/code/tests and sibling callers to falsify the claim. - Independence boundary: separate session/role; no code writes, no author-to-reviewer routing; judgment belongs to reviewer, corrective acceptance to lead. - Who acts on findings: lead decides hold/repair and developer revises, with reviewer re-engaged after new commits.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied externally prospective adaptation dialogue with current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing future technology/user environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific prospective adaptation judgment established. - Decision owner: none established; process-improvement holder has current pipeline duties. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: process records, trials, pipeline measurements and role rules. - Closure path: internal pipeline improvement/record rulings are not a demonstrated outside-and-then capability loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: roles and measurements do not by themselves establish S4; positive/parent alternatives were checked first. - Evidence: docs/roles/process-improvement.md; METHOD.md; docs/roles/delivery-lead.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime ultimate identity-policy adjudication path at the configured team boundary established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity/ultimate-policy conflict between operations and adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: owner&#39;s product/budget permissions and standing rules do not alone specify identity-level closure. - Decision owner: no qualifying S5 decision arrangement established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS, dated owner words, DECISIONS and role permissions. - Closure path: ordinary task/resource/process decisions return through the lead; no distinct ultimate-policy tension and resolution-return arrangement supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: budget authority is S3 here; prompts, configuration and generic owner final say are not promoted to S5. - Evidence: AGENTS.md; docs/roles/delivery-lead.md; docs/roles/process-improvement.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0ef1403f338bc0592268db9928d9ea1921ee390b/assessments/qu/qujck-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0ef1403f338bc0592268db9928d9ea1921ee390b\">0ef1403 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T18:04:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ominiverdi/ferrum\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ferrum</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ominiverdi/ferrum/tree/f31151d34fade3ef88104a33ba2a4042e689be35\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f31151d34fade3ef88104a33ba2a4042e689be35\">f31151d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work on the selected workspace by interpreting the task, inspecting project evidence, selecting coding/tool actions, applying or running them and reacting to returned results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous source trees and project instructions, incomplete task information, file contents, search/command output, tool failures, changing workspace state and implementation choices encountered while completing coding work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what project evidence to inspect, which exposed coding action/tool to invoke next, what modifications or commands to attempt, and when enough work/evidence exists to produce the final response within externally selected policy limits. - Decision owner: the model-backed coding actor instantiated by Ferrum&#39;s standard agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime/system/project context construction; native/MCP tool registry; provider adapters; safety/tool/root policy; durable session/history; compaction; loop guard; cancellation; interactive/print/ACP transport. - Closure path: user task/workspace context → provider request → model selects tool/action → Ferrum executes or rejects the action → tool/environment result is appended to the session context → the same model-backed actor receives the result and selects the next action or terminates. - Boundary reachability: ferrum, ferrum -p and each supported ACP session directly instantiate the first-party agent/tool loop; no application-authored orchestration layer is needed to reach the coding actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor while retaining tools, policies and session machinery removes the open-ended coding judgment that selects and sequences repository-facing work. - Evidence: docs/spec.md; docs/tools.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider/model inference remains an external dependency; Ferrum is credited for the shipped role/tool/feedback composition, not provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function was established at the selected recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S2 level because one active Ferrum agent session exposes one primary model-backed S1 rather than multiple distinct S1 operational units with a concrete mutual interference/oscillation relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safe read-only tool batches may execute concurrently; mutating/mixed/MCP batches stay sequential; ACP can host multiple independent sessions; session persistence uses file locking. These are execution/session mechanisms rather than a demonstrated coordination relation among S1 units inside one organization. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct-S1 disturbance → attenuation → changed subsequent S1 behavior loop exists in the reviewed standard session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel read/ls/grep/find calls are tools chosen by the same coding actor. Concurrent ACP sessions are documented as independent agent sessions, so their co-residence in one server process does not create subordinate S1s at the selected session recursion. - Evidence: docs/tools.md; docs/acp.md; docs/spec.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this classification is recursion-sensitive; a separately defined external organization composed from several Ferrum ACP sessions could require a different assessment, but Ferrum does not supply that organization at the reviewed boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-organization current-control function distinct from the coding S1 and deterministic loop/runtime safeguards was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S3 ownership level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. The adaptive loop guard can detect repeated calls/errors and alter the next request, while cancellation, tool-round limits and policy can stop/limit work; these mechanisms regulate one S1&#39;s execution according to encoded/operator policy rather than manage current commitments/resources across an organization of S1 units. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adaptive loop guard; optional maxtoolrounds; cancellation; safety tiers; tool/root policy; session/ACP active-turn limits; foreground wait bounds. - Closure path: not applicable; no whole-organization current view plus substantive resource/priority/commitment/accountability intervention path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the loop guard&#39;s response is deterministic runtime policy and survives conceptually without an autonomous management actor; model action after guard guidance remains the same S1&#39;s operational continuation rather than S3 management. - Evidence: docs/tools.md; docs/spec.md; docs/acp.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong lifecycle/safety enforcement is not denied; it does not meet the Profile&#39;s stronger S3 organizational-control threshold at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary audit role with sufficiently independent access, audit judgment and corrective return into the supported runtime was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S3 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Ferrum can run tests/commands as normal S1 tools and its repository includes benchmark validators, but the standard coding distribution does not wire a separate reviewer/judge actor to challenge S1 conclusions and return an audit verdict into current control. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary bash/tool verification, deterministic safety validation and repository bench/ task validators are operation/development evidence rather than an independent runtime audit owner. - Closure path: not applicable; no supported independent challenge → audit judgment → corrective-control return path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a coding model may choose to run tests or inspect its own work, but that remains S1 self-checking. Repository benchmark scripts are adjacent project-development evaluation and are not boundary-reachable decision owners in ordinary Ferrum sessions. - Evidence: README.md; docs/tools.md; bench/. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic validation and self-verification can materially improve quality without satisfying S3 independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective environment-intelligence and organizational adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Ferrum can load current project instructions/skills, consult session history, use MCP/tools and summarize old context, but these mechanisms support the present coding operation rather than model future environmental change and persistently alter Ferrum&#39;s organizational capability/strategy. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS/context files; skills; MCP; history search/read; durable sessions; context compaction; user/provider/model configuration; foreground wait monitoring. - Closure path: not applicable; no prospective scan → adaptation-option formation → persistent capability/strategy change → later operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: compaction changes the representation of current conversation history, and history/skills expose information to S1. The separate background-task document explicitly states that durable/model-owned background tasks are deferred exploration rather than implemented behavior. - Evidence: docs/spec.md; docs/tools.md; docs/background-tasks.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: present-task learning/replanning and persistent conversational state are not sufficient S4 evidence under Profile 0.2.4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. User/global/project configuration chooses provider/model, system prompt, safety, tool/root/MCP/skill limits and the task itself; project policy may narrow authority but cannot autonomously redefine Ferrum&#39;s identity/purpose. - Decision owner: not established inside the assessed organization; ultimate purpose/policy remains developer/operator/project-config owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safety tiers; [tools] allow/deny and writable roots; project restrictive policy; system prompt override; ACP optional permission UX; credential/protected-target guards; cancellation and tool limits. - Closure path: not applicable; no identity/ultimate-policy proposal/dispute reaches an autonomous or qualifying parent S5 decision path and returns as an authoritative policy change to later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime strongly enforces selected limits, but enforcement is distinct from ownership. ACP client permission can only restrict an operation Ferrum already authorizes and is ordinary operational approval, not an identity-level parent governance loop. - Evidence: README.md; docs/spec.md; docs/acp.md; docs/tools.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: restrictive project policy and client permissions are important governance/safety boundaries, but generic configuration/approval does not satisfy S5 identity closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d1313c4f47597c6c7244423827f1de5b7f68f669/assessments/ferrum.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d1313c4f47597c6c7244423827f1de5b7f68f669\">d1313c4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:33:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fenix46/LuckyCLI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LuckyCLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fenix46/LuckyCLI/tree/496c89124c9a349c36337df35df6581533e26581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 496c89124c9a349c36337df35df6581533e26581\">496c891 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute a coding request through iterative native model-directed file/command/search/graph actions and optional independently operated subagent subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes in requested repository work, incomplete context, tool errors, failed verification, codebase structure, model errors and return reports from delegated subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next first-party tool/delegated task, respond to its result, alter the work path, and decide when the current request is sufficiently complete. - Decision owner: model running within first-party Agent.send of the root or a separately instantiated sequential subordinate Agent. The local provider supplies inference but does not own the tool orchestration or terminal workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, model transport, transcript/context compaction, permission callback, subagent profile registry, subagent bridge, graph enrichment and ordinary test checks. - Closure path: user request → model stream with tool calls → local first-party dispatcher → filesystem/shell/graph or child-agent report → concrete tool result appended to active conversation → model chooses another action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: packages/cli/src/runtime.ts builds the main Agent for installed CLI and ACP/TUI; packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts directly executes the tool/result continuation; Root.tsx creates a reachable child delegation bridge. No third-party coding-agent process is required for the owned S1 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s chosen tool call/delegation and interpretation of returned observations, runtime still enforces safety and verifies scripts but cannot autonomously decide the next coding operation. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts; packages/cli/src/runtime.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/Root.tsx; packages/core/src/agents/runner.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/spawn-agent.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/index.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high from first-party loop implementations. - Caveats: evidence establishes a native operational feedback right but not live benchmark performance or universal working provider credentials.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no native inter-S1 interference/oscillation regulation relation among simultaneously active first-party operational units was established at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: subagent write conflicts are avoided by not running peers at the same time; there is no separate active multi-worker collision that a dedicated coordinating mechanism observes and attenuates with feedback to both agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main agent chooses the next sequential delegate; the runner completes one child before returning. No peer-claim arbitration, shared multiple-writer lock, cross-agent collision feedback or active conflict attenuation decision owner is supported. - Decision owner: root operational task agent decides delegation, while deterministic child invocation serializes; neither establishes S2-specific feedback across concurrently active S1s. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static serial subagent runner, child tools exclusion list, task list, tool permission limits and request/response child summaries. - Closure path: root awaits completion of one child → report returned → next task selected by the root as an S1 action; no distinct inter-unit disturbances or compensating S2 loop are presented to active peers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-guided sequencing of one current child is part of the operational task and does not become distinct S2 coordination merely because the tool is called spawnagent. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agents/runner.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/spawn-agent.ts; packages/core/src/tasks/store.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/tasks.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: if external users run several installations concurrently, their external coordination is not a native first-party LuckyCLI function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independently owned whole-current organization oversight of simultaneously operating S1 units, commitments and resources was established beyond one user&#39;s coding objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the root may split a project into serial subtasks, track local work progress and choose different providers, but that is one S1&#39;s delegated implementation rather than system-wide management of multiple ongoing operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a named child profile/task, update the local task list and request user approvals; none is a demonstrated whole-current authority over an independent operational workforce. - Decision owner: the root coder for current task progress, user for consent. No separate first-party S3 management decision right at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local tasks, plans, sequential delegations, checkpoint/snapshot and review tool outputs, usage/status panel. - Closure path: child completion or tool error → root task agent continues ordinary work; no distinct current-organization decision over multiple active S1 resource/commitment obligations is returned to those units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: although a model chooses local work order, it does not exercise whole-current control over an operational organization separate from the one coding task. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agents/runner.ts; packages/core/src/agent/plan.ts; packages/core/src/tasks/store.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/tasks.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/Root.tsx; packages/cli/src/ui/components/TaskPanel.tsx. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: generic task planning and model/provider configuration do not establish S3; an external project manager may coordinate multiple independent LuckyCLI instances outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit judgment/alternative access path that returns findings to a distinct current-control owner is established in this first-party coding installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: generated code may fail tests or differ from changed Git diffs; those risks are addressed by ordinary first-party coding verification or user-initiated review and do not establish a separate complementary organizational inquiry. - Decisive decision or feedback right: current coding loop can choose a verify tool or run configured after-edit tests; the user can invoke explicit review/verify commands, but no independent audit actor takes an alternative evidentiary mandate and returns an S3 finding to a metasystemic current decision owner. - Decision owner: project scripts and in-band model supply tests/review text; the producing root agent and/or user decide subsequent ordinary task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trusted project-script execution, structured result/exit code, mandatory re-entry to the tool transcript after editing when enabled, diff collection, JSON review schema, manual /review and lucky review. - Closure path: edit → ordinary script/check status appended to that very tool result (or user-initiated diff review in the same model&#39;s session) → coder may retry the failing edit. This is useful testing but not a sufficiently independent, complementary audit-control path beyond routine production checks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct raw tests can contradict a current edit&#39;s apparent success, but after-edit is an in-band routine QA hook with no independent auditor selecting substantive evidence or returning a distinct whole-current audit finding. The manual lucky review command presents findings to its caller; it does not wire an S3→S3 control return into ongoing agent execution. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/verify.ts; packages/core/src/workflow/verify.ts; packages/core/src/workflow/review.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/commands/workflow.ts; packages/cli/src/workflow-cli.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/index.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a claim that separate human review or tests are unnecessary or ineffective, only that their witnessed ownership/return does not establish the Profile&#39;s organizational S3 at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective outside-and-then process that creates future capability adaptation alternatives and returns them to current control is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future provider/model availability, changing source structure and codebase knowledge may matter to the developer, but LuckyCLI code reviewed uses them to improve current task context and route immediate inference/tool operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: models choose current coding work and provider configurations; deterministic graph index and skill matching gather relevant current repository facts. No owned long-horizon environmental intelligence decision or adaptation-option/return is found. - Decision owner: current task coder or user for model updates and settings, not a separate first-party S4 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project knowledge graph build/query/update, skill graph matching, context enrichment, update policy/staging, provider catalog and session history. - Closure path: updated graph facts or installed user-selected release influence current/future prompt context, but no external prospective investigation → adaptation choice → S3 system capability adjustment loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent memory, code graph refresh and user-initiated software updates are helpful facilities but not prospective organizational intelligence in the VSM sense. - Evidence: packages/core/src/graph/enrich.ts; packages/core/src/graph/update.ts; packages/core/src/skills/graph.ts; packages/core/src/update/policy.ts; packages/cli/src/update.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: project users may use the tool to research future options, but such tasks alone are not native ongoing system-level adaptation authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party highest-authority identity/ethos decision and binding governance return exists at the installed agent organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local tool risk, provider permissions, user trust and session approval, updates and model selection are handled by configured execution constraints instead of an ultimate-identity dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human user grants/denies a tool action, software enforces tool permission and approved command scopes; no discretionary S3–S4 identity-level resolution with durable returned policy. - Decision owner: user for ordinary consent and provider/config settings, deterministic runtime for enforcement, not a verified first-party S5 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool permission policy, approveTool callback, remembered approvals, session trust prompt, plan presentation and update rules. - Closure path: action request is allowed/denied and current coding operation continues; no exceptional mission/identity issue reaches a legitimate highest-level authority and returns a binding system-level rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary approval and persistent configuration are not policy-identity arbitration; no parent governance is inferred solely from user ownership of settings. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts; packages/cli/src/approval.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/TrustPrompt.tsx; packages/core/src/agent/plan.ts; packages/core/src/update/policy.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human/enterprise organization may govern installations externally; its policy may constrain the program but that does not create a first-party S5 return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5bf3826fa02de7383eec5063ace990630eb82dc3/assessments/luckycli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5bf3826fa02de7383eec5063ace990630eb82dc3\">5bf3826 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T03:05:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/reflex-agent/reflex-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reflex</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/reflex-agent/reflex-agent/tree/f3b3dd7b438c8333e5731a29023eca81239c7d19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f3b3dd7b438c8333e5731a29023eca81239c7d19\">f3b3dd7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-driven execution of user/project work through persistent Reflex topics, delegated specialist turns, tasks and scheduled/headless runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user requests, project/file state, tool results, specialist findings, task progress, permissions and workflow-triggered work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how to pursue the requested outcome within the Reflex topic, including whether/how to delegate specialist work, what returned evidence to synthesize and which first-party action markers to emit. - Decision owner: autonomous model-driven operating actor running under Reflex&#39;s first-party orchestrator protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent topics/events, specialist dispatch protocol, task records, bounded /goal continuation, worktree cwd binding, host tools, permissions and headless execution. - Closure path: model decision → Reflex action/dispatch/tool execution → changed project/task/environment state or child-agent result → observation/reinvocation → subsequent model decision/output. - Boundary reachability: default interactive topics directly reach the Reflex orchestrator turn; task dispatch and scheduled/headless workflows also reach the same first-party operating path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: once an operating turn starts, the model actor has bounded discretion over decomposition/delegation/action and consumes returned results; Reflex supplies and closes that organizational protocol. Claude Code/Codex may execute the model/tool substrate, but no internal metasystem semantics from those external subprocesses are imported. - Evidence: lib/reflex/prompts/defaults.ts; lib/server/agents/headless.ts; lib/server/tasks/dispatch.ts; topic/goal manager path. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the positive claim is Reflex&#39;s first-party operating organization around the model actor, not a claim that Claude Code/Codex internals belong to Reflex.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between concurrent code-task S1 units that would otherwise mutate the same repository checkout/files. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent agent tasks operating on one repository can collide through shared file mutations, branch/checkout state and commits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a disturbance-specific coordination arrangement that separates concurrent code-task mutation lanes before task execution begins. - Decision owner: Reflex supplies and deterministically enforces the worktree isolation constructor path; no autonomous first-party coordinator owns discretionary S2 policy across competing units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: unique task/&lt;slug&gt; branch/worktree allocation, destination/branch collision checks, worktree-bound task cwd and explicit later merge path. - Closure path: code task selected → Reflex creates isolated worktree/branch → dispatched task agent is instructed and launched inside that checkout → subsequent file operations/commits occur outside main/shared checkout → later controlled merge reconciles output. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination function and closure are first-party, but the decisive isolation policy is deterministic host enforcement rather than an autonomous coordinator that observes competing units and chooses/adjusts coordination policy. This supports C, not A. - Evidence: lib/server/tasks/worktree.ts; lib/server/tasks/dispatch.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic multi-agent dispatch, dependency fields and shared task state are not separately counted as S2. - Distinct S1 units: separate task-bound model/agent executions can concurrently perform code work against the same project repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: tasks/worktree.ts explicitly states that each code task runs in its own checkout so tasks do not collide on files; a shared checkout would expose one task to another task&#39;s mutations/branch state. - Attenuating coordination relation: Reflex allocates a unique worktree and branch per code task before starting the task agent, thereby separating mutation lanes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: dispatchTask binds the agent cwd to the isolated worktree and prompts it to commit there, so later S1 file operations are changed by the coordination decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the evidence names a concrete cross-S1 file-collision disturbance and a first-party isolation response whose purpose is to prevent that disturbance, rather than merely routing or delegating work. - Boundary reachability: standard task dispatch automatically reaches the worktree-isolation path for code tasks in Git repositories.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: whole-Space current supervision and intervention over ongoing commitments, running agent work and blocked permission decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous current commitments, runaway/stale work, blocked agent turns, requested privileges and operational intervention needs across a project Space. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether current goals/work continue, are stopped/abandoned, and whether requested permissions are allowed or denied. - Decision owner: legitimate parent user/owner operating the first-party Reflex control surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: root dashboard aggregation, active-goal/running-agent views, pending-approval aggregation, agent-manager stop path, goal clearing, permission response bridge and explicit task/worktree merge controls. - Closure path: Reflex surfaces whole-Space current work or a blocked decision → user selects stop/abandon/allow/deny or enters the relevant work item for intervention → Reflex manager/store applies the decision → waiting/running S1 operation changes or terminates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reflex supplies the whole-Space view and enforces returned interventions, but no autonomous supervisor is established with equivalent authority to reprioritize/stop/authorize work across the Space. The parent user owns the decisive S3 right. - Evidence: lib/server/dashboard-actions.ts; app/roots/[id]/components/dashboard-active-goals.tsx; app/roots/[id]/components/dashboard-pending-approvals.tsx; lib/server/topic-actions.ts; lib/server/agents/manager.ts; app/api/agents/[agentId]/respond/route.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: local topic decisions and ordinary orchestrator delegation remain S1 activity; they are not promoted to S3 by the manager or dispatcher names. - Whole-system current view: the root dashboard aggregates active persistent goals, currently running agents without goals and pending permission/question/MCP interactions across every topic of the Space, giving the parent a cross-operating-unit current view. - Current-control decision scope: stop/delete running topics, abandon persistent goals, answer blocked questions, allow/deny requested operations and retain explicit control over merging task work into the main repository state. - Boundary reachability: these are ordinary first-party dashboard/chat/runtime surfaces in the standard distribution, and permission decisions are returned through the live manager to waiting agent execution.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no qualifying complementary independent audit function is established at the declared Reflex Space boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for operational claims or conditions that are independently checked through a distinct evidence path rather than merely logged by the same runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent auditor was found that judges discrepancies and returns them into S3 current control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: utility audit JSONL, agent event logs, task observation/status and dashboard aggregation provide observability but not the required independent audit relation. - Closure path: host/API calls can be logged and later viewed, but no distinct ordinary-path claim → complementary reality check → audit judgment → corrective current-control feedback loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the utility audit is emitted by the same host execution path it records; neither it nor event/status presentation establishes an independently judging audit actor. - Evidence: lib/server/utilities/audit.ts; app/audit/page.tsx; lib/server/tasks/observe.ts; dashboard/event surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: auditability/logging is useful evidence infrastructure but is not equivalent to S3 organizational ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no complete first-party outside-and-then environmental intelligence/adaptation loop is established at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for external/future-relevant change that is deliberately sensed, converted into adaptation options, decided upon and returned into current capability/operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is established with a closed right to choose prospective capability adaptation from external/future distinctions and install that choice into subsequent operating repertoire. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow scheduler, headless agent execution, generated utilities, searchable memory, weekly memory rollup and on-demand dashboard AI suggestions can support operation/adaptation but do not establish the required S4 closure themselves. - Closure path: configured workflows execute and memory/suggestions summarize internal/past/current state, but no evidenced external/future sensing → adaptation option → decision → present capability change → later S1/S3 use loop is closed by the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model suggestions and utility generation may be model-driven, but at the pinned revision their evidenced triggers concern current user/project needs and require composition/user action; they do not establish an agent-owned prospective environmental-intelligence loop. - Evidence: lib/server/workflows/scheduler.ts; lib/server/workflows/system-tasks.ts; lib/server/ai-suggestions.ts; utility generation/update/host surfaces inspected in the pinned tree. - Basis: structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream user-authored workflows could create an S4 loop, but each such specialization is a separate evidence/boundary question and is not inherited by the standalone repository.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no qualifying ultimate-policy or system-identity closure is established for the Reflex Space organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for questions about the Space&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity/policy that are distinguished from ordinary operating configuration and permission choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path identifies such an identity/policy issue, routes it to an actor with explicit ultimate authority and closes the returned decision into subsequent organizational identity. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt templates, model assignments, permission settings, workflow definitions and project settings constrain operation but remain configuration/support surfaces. - Closure path: users can edit prompts/settings and approve tools, but no evidenced identity issue → ultimate-policy judgment → durable identity update → later organizational behavior loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no autonomous or parent-governed S5 actor/path is established; ordinary owner configuration is not upgraded to S5 merely because it affects behavior. - Evidence: lib/reflex/prompts/defaults.ts; settings/model assignment surfaces; permission response path; workflow/task controls; project dashboard surfaces. - Basis: structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream Space may embed identity policy in user-authored prompts/workflows, but that downstream specialization is outside this standalone repository assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3d75bbc6fa7ba5292840a4d022d6622838c7acdc/assessments/reflex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3d75bbc6fa7ba5292840a4d022d6622838c7acdc\">3d75bbc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T20:55:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohaquinlop/michin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MichiN</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohaquinlop/michin/tree/ff001031aad8486583055455989e02d2a88c8518\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ff001031aad8486583055455989e02d2a88c8518\">ff00103 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute first-party model-selected source and shell tools for project coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user requirements, source state, compile errors, returned tool failures and environmental constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select contingent source-edit/read/search/bash actions and decide subsequent work after observing outcomes. - Decision owner: The coding LLM operating through MichiN&#39;s own Agent/loop, with action rights bounded by native tool/policy enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider streaming, native tool dispatcher, guardrails, context history, session state and user approval/mode choices. - Closure path: User coding request → inference chooses tool action → first-party native tool executes on workspace → returned result enters core state → model chooses next corrective action or finishes. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI, TUI, prompt and RPC operation call the first-party Rust agent core and first-party concrete tools. External providers supply inference only, not an external coding harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides how to absorb local coding variety; native tools and policies enforce its chosen bounded work. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs; crates/michin/src/tools/write.rs; crates/michin/src/tools/bash.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is evidence of operative autonomy, not a measured benchmark-quality claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party organizational coordination loop across two independently functioning operational S1 cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent tool execution may affect one active coding session, but does not evidence competing autonomous work units whose variety must be mutually stabilized. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None across distinct S1 units; generic tool execution mode is selected/obeyed inside the single coder. - Decision owner: No separate S2 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parallel or sequential native tool execution, queued user messages, session-level cancellation. - Closure path: Tool output returns to the one model/agent; no inter-S1 interference detection and feedback to another S1 is seen. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel file reads, serialized edit calls, model switching and queueing do not constitute an inter-agent coordination relationship. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Separately running MichiN processes would need a separately established first-party organizational connection.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned whole-current regulator that supervises and reallocates work across multiple operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Run interruption, latency, current budget, repeated model/tool requests and execution errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User steers or cancels; native runtime enforces timeout/repetition bounds and local safety policy. - Decision owner: Deterministic single-session runtime and human task operator, not an S3 actor with discretionary whole-current view. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Active-run mutex, cancellation tokens, run reports, tool watchdog, turn termination statuses and model escalation. - Closure path: Stop/steer/escalate changes this agent&#39;s present tool turn, without manager-mediated system-wide current allocation across multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Status reports, execution ceilings and individual agent control do not transfer broader managerial judgment to the model. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/events.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External human management of a coding team lies outside this packaged local session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material independent operational-audit owner with corrective return to coding S1. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe commands and possibly defective code require checks, but safety enforcement does not itself establish complementary audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic command safety rejects destructive actions; optional Rhai scripts can pre-block or post-observe tools, not act as a separately autonomous supplementary auditor by default. - Decision owner: Static runtime policy and user-configured scripts; original coder retains responsibility for correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Before/after tool hooks, Rhai executor, command policy engine and tool result events. - Closure path: Hook/policy may block one tool, or script observes a result; errors return to original coding context. No separate source-reading auditor returns independent defect judgment as binding corrective work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Custom hooks, a second plan model and tool guards are not automatically complementary audit actors. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin-script/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A separately supplied Rhai extension might implement richer audit logic, but its hypothetical behavior cannot be borrowed as default first-party capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment analysis and strategic organizational capability renewal authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-horizon external requirements and future tool/provider evolution are not addressed by one-session task planning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User selects model/plan track, preserves skills and restores sessions; the single coding agent performs local plans. - Decision owner: No first-party prospective S4 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan and build model tracks, context compaction, persistent session forks, resources and skills. - Closure path: Settings and notes guide later coding turns, but no future-environment options are examined and accepted into organizational capabilities by a strategic owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory, switching to a better model and proposing a coding plan are not standalone future-environment intelligence. - Evidence: crates/michin/src/settings.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/compact.rs; crates/michin/src/skills.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer research/roadmap decisions are outside installed runtime ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No constitutive identity/ultimate-purpose policy authority with organizational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission risk and destructive commands are local execution safety matters, not highest-order identity/policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User configures policies and model mode; runtime follows explicit forbidden-command constraints. - Decision owner: Human operator for task settings and deterministic guard for enforcement; no legitimate highest-level first-party actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Command policy, strict safety flags, Rhai allow/block callbacks and plan-mode mutation gate. - Closure path: Tool permitted/refused or model track changed; no ultimate organizational identity-policy decision returns to regulate the whole operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Constraints and human approvals do not establish an autonomous S5 decision right. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin/src/config.rs; crates/michin/src/settings.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: OSS repository governance, licensing and developer policy are adjacent to user coding session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5e6d9710b191304c3ba49f7a9abe9f75447e3f50/assessments/michin.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5e6d9710b191304c3ba49f7a9abe9f75447e3f50\">5e6d971 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T21:40:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fennec-AI/agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fennec Agent Runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fennec-AI/agent-runtime/tree/3441fbde350df50cc070851856c343680babefb4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3441fbde350df50cc070851856c343680babefb4\">3441fbd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select model-guided actions and tool arguments for an assigned user task, observe their effects or errors and iteratively choose the next action or terminal response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novel user instructions, file/tool observations, tool execution errors, rejected permissions, child task results and context-length growth. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ctx.llm model in the shipped query loop emits toolcalls or final text and is fed the actual ToolMessage and child-inbox observations on the next iteration. - Decision owner: Configured model acting through first-party Session/ query and runtools boundary; LangChain/provider services transport inference and host sets tool grants but do not displace model selection of the next substantive task action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party registry and RunContext, safety-filtered tool adapter, BaseChatModel.bindtools, tool executor, child notifications, compaction, cancellation, events, user approval callback and typed terminal result facility. - Closure path: Task → Session.submitmessage / query → llm.bindtools(...).astream → model-selected tool → runtools permission check and tool.ainvoke → ToolMessage in history or child completion notification → same model re-evaluates → terminal result. - Boundary reachability: The public Session facade invokes the shipped query and its tool-dispatch branch; query feeds concrete returned tool results into the next model call and SpawnAgentTool reuses it for children. The README quickstart connects the actual packaged Session, bootstrap, defaulttools() and provider model, not an externally invented loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model, the first-party tool dispatcher and safety infrastructure can execute preselected actions but cannot choose task-sensitive tool calls from new observations. The model owns the material S1 decision, bounded by host-selected tool grants. - Evidence: README.md#L72, agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/session.py#L89, agentruntime/query.py#L110, agentruntime/query.py#L181, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L102, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/tools/spawnagent.py#L83. - Basis: Concrete shipped model-call/tool-output return wiring, not extrapolation from README feature labels alone. - Confidence: High for source-connected operating capability; no local provider inference validation. - Caveats: External LangChain/provider and valid API key/runtime dependencies are needed to execute real tasks; a source-based A does not imply measured reliability or autonomy beyond the configured operating cell.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate source-backed regulator of specific interference/oscillation between independently viable S1 operating divisions with altered reciprocal operating commitments is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent child tasks may compete for a shared worktree or return conflicting content; async notifications may arrive during parent streaming, but those issues are not the same as interference between independent business S1 systems. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent operating model selects subtask calls and may send messages, poll or cancel; runtime has deterministic safe-tool batching and inbox delivery. Neither path senses concrete inter-S1 department conflict, decides dampening, and returns binding mutual adjustment to two viable operating units. - Decision owner: Same parent operating model / host task instructions for delegation, process-local routing and task cancellation code for transport/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SpawnAgentTool, TaskOutput, TaskStop, SendMessage, AGENTREGISTRY, inbox drain and concurrency semaphore, cooperative cancellation. - Closure path: Parent delegates → child returns or notifies → parent continues current user task; no multi-S1 shared operational interference → distinct coordinating judgment → changed independent operating commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model mediation of child tasks is still the parent S1 operating decision. A messenger, task graph or transport does not automatically confer the S2 function. - Evidence: agentruntime/tools/spawnagent.py#L1, agentruntime/query.py#L143, agentruntime/query.py#L210, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L102, agentruntime/registry.py#L1. - Basis: Source-bounded negative after inspecting real child task, process-inbox and safe-batch modes. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared one-goal operating recursion. - Caveats: A downstream application could install multiple independently viable S1 agents and new conflict-regulating logic; it would require its own evidence and recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current management owner of a portfolio of independently viable S1 operations that reviews present obligations/resources and sends binding managerial adjustments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Depth/max-turn constraints, cancellation, shell timeout, active child tasks, approval and context budget threaten single-task execution, but not whole-organizational current performance accountability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/host configures permission mode, tools, model and maxturns; parent model selects child task instructions, and runtime applies fixed deterministic run limits. No source-backed separate managerial owner negotiates resource allocations or current organizational commitments. - Decision owner: Application operator for delegated bounds and parent operating model for subtask choices; deterministic runtime for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session, RunContext, CONFIG, TaskStop, abort tree, registry, permission callback and structured output checking. - Closure path: Task or tool requests action → configured permission/depth/abort gate applies → same task continues or ends. No whole-current manager&#39;s operating overview → change in S1 portfolio priorities/resources → returned operational adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A class called Session or parent worker does not by itself own discretionary current management of a viable multi-unit organization. - Evidence: agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/query.py#L102, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/cancellation.py#L1, agentruntime/config.py#L1. - Basis: Source-bounded negative for registered source modes, including child and approval routes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Human S3 or autonomous management could exist externally at a higher application boundary; not imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary operational-reality inspection of S1/S3 claims with a corrective feedback path to a current S3 executive is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool errors, unsafe actions and malformed typed answers are observable and may be denied or retried. Those observations do not constitute independent challenge of an S3 operating picture. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluatepermission may call an external approval callback; hooks log/observe tool use; structured output Pydantic validation checks schema during the same task. No separate first-party S3 correction mandate is wired. - Decision owner: Host approval callback and deterministic tool/typed response checks; not a complementary independent auditor with rights over a current S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool-level pretool/ posttool/ onerror hooks, permissiondenied events, runstructured, transcript history, registry/cancellation. - Closure path: Tool result or approval decision → returned ToolMessage / validation action / log → current S1 loop; no independent reality sample → corrective S3 audit finding → revised S3 current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission review and output validation are local action controls, not S3 regardless of their security value. - Evidence: agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/permissions.py#L1, agentruntime/hooks.py#L1, agentruntime/session.py#L107. - Basis: Function-first negative: checked concrete validation and hook/approval closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A downstream independent evidence checker could leverage the logs, but no such authority is credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No model-owned proactive future environment distinction, prospective organizational adaptation option and adoption back to changed durable future operating capability is evidenced in the shipped default or alternate documented modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long conversations, differing tool results and future continuation after a child completes can require local context adjustment, but not a durable new strategy for a later independent operational cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autocompactifneeded may ask the configured model to summarize the current history and replace messages at a context threshold; Session persists current messages; TodoWrite updates current task checklist. None owns prospective changes to installed tools, goals, model policies or future agent skill capabilities. - Decision owner: Same operating loop&#39;s compaction / task model and application for any later configuration changes; no distinct S4 adaptation right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compaction/autocompact.py, microcompact.py, token budget, messages and action history, child completion notifications, TODO list tool. - Closure path: Growing current transcript → compact summary in same messages list → same model continues current task; missing prospective future environmental distinction → approved strategy change → reused operational capability loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-written conversation summaries are still local operating-context maintenance, not strategy ownership. No first-party future-option development and adoption is returned into later independent runs. - Evidence: agentruntime/query.py#L74, agentruntime/compaction/autocompact.py#L47, agentruntime/session.py#L89, agentruntime/tools/todowrite.py#L1, README.md#L24. - Basis: Bounded negative after reviewing persistent session/child and compaction modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Host developers could expose a model-written durable skill/update tool, but that would be a new application-owned construction and is not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate first-party ultimate-purpose/identity decision authority with returned enduring binding organizational policy is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potentially destructive shell/file tools, elevated permission modes and background subagent lifecycle require user approval/limits, but no constitutional identity-level dispute is admitted or decided. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host user supplies permission mode and optional askcallback; runtime checks allow/deny/ask for tool actions. bypassPermissions is a caller-selected setting, not first-party S5 policy-making authority. - Decision owner: External user/application actor for authorization; deterministic runtime evaluates delegated boundaries and operating model chooses permitted task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionMode, AskCallback, per-tool allow/deny, tool/child cancellation, logs and optional hooks. - Closure path: Tool requests action → policy gate/host callback → tool executes or denial returned to current agent; no ultimate identity question reaches a legitimate authority and returns as binding constitutional policy governing future task contexts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even a high-friction human gate is not itself a functioning organizational S5 policy/identity constitution. - Evidence: agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/permissions.py#L1, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, README.md#L31. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking all named permission modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External human owners retain real final purpose/policy rights; no internally self-deciding S5 organization is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/13dab0d4f2954614f7f99a9fa044ad5b9a154490/assessments/fe/fennec-agent-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 13dab0d4f2954614f7f99a9fa044ad5b9a154490\">13dab0d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T19:10:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bottrall/riffer-rig\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">riffer-rig</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bottrall/riffer-rig/tree/e2e914195f7c15c75ee2ae6bb55d772d754a2f37\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e2e914195f7c15c75ee2ae6bb55d772d754a2f37\">e2e9141 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work through model-selected repository inspection, edits, writes and shell execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, incomplete task information, file state, failing commands/tests, ambiguous edit targets, model/provider outputs and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding/tool action, interpret tool errors/results and revise subsequent actions until the requested coding outcome is reached. - Decision owner: the active model-backed riffer-rig CodingAgent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CodingAgent, generic-agent execution instantiated by the rig, read/write/edit/bash tools, REPL streaming, runtime cancel flag, hooks/guardrails, AGENTS.md prompt section and provider/model configuration. - Closure path: user/project context → model selects action/tool → tool executes against the working directory → result/error returns to the agent context → model changes subsequent action until completion or cancellation. - Boundary reachability: the installed riffer executable directly instantiates the repository-owned CodingAgent and bundled coding tools; no adjacent development workflow or unconfigured generic runtime feature is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves tools, UI and runtime wrappers but removes the open-ended decisions that turn repository evidence into coding actions. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/codingagent.rb; lib/riffer/rig/repl.rb; lib/riffer/rig/tools/read.rb; write.rb; edit.rb; bash.rb. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the generic riffer execution kernel is a dependency; only behavior concretely instantiated by the frozen riffer-rig composition is credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct coordination function among multiple S1 operational units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the focal runtime may execute tools and load extensions/skills, but the standard coding application exposes one model-backed coding unit rather than peer S1 units with an interference relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific choice over inter-S1 conflict, oscillation or mutual adjustment was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime busy state, cancellation, extension ordering and per-runtime working directory. - Closure path: these mechanisms constrain one operational session and do not feed a coordination result back into distinct peer S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: serialization, extension ordering and tool composition do not establish S2 without a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/runtime.rb; docs/TOOLS.md; docs/SESSIONS.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: embedders can construct multiple runtimes externally, but the frozen first-party rig does not supply a peer-coordination relation among them.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cancellation, runtime busy/closed state, tool allowlists and request/tool guardrails constrain current execution within one session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate owner has a whole-system current view and substantive authority over shared resources, commitments, priorities or interventions across multiple operations. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime busy/closed state, cancel flag, request/tool guardrails, hooks, model switching and host notifications. - Closure path: these controls alter or stop the focal session&#39;s current action path but do not form a whole-current metasystemic loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local execution controls and guardrails are enforcement around S1, not whole-system management. - Evidence: lib/riffer/rig/runtime.rb; runtime/requestguardrail.rb; runtime hooks; REPL model switching. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an embedding application could add fleet management, but such an adjacent composition is outside this concrete coding rig.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool results, command/test failures, hooks and repository tests can reveal mistakes, but they remain ordinary production feedback or adjacent development checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate reviewer/evaluator owns an independent claim-checking judgment that returns findings into a current-control path. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell/test output, after-tool hooks, notifications and repository test suite. - Closure path: runtime evidence returns to the same coding agent or host; no distinct complementary-access audit loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-checking via bash/tests and hook observation are ordinary operational evidence, not S3. - Evidence: lib/riffer/rig/tools/bash.rb; runtime hooks/events; repository tests. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an extension could implement review behavior, but no such first-party review path is instantiated in the frozen standard rig.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AGENTS.md, user/project skills, provider/model settings and snapshots can change the context used by later turns or restored sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party process senses environmental/future change, generates adaptation options for the rig and returns a selected adaptation into current capability. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live AGENTS.md loading, filesystem skill backend, model/provider configuration and runtime snapshot/restore. - Closure path: operator-authored instructions/configuration or saved session state are loaded into later operation; no autonomous external-and-prospective adaptation conversation exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context loading and extensibility reuse externally supplied material but do not autonomously develop future-oriented adaptation options. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/prompts/agentsmd.rb; lib/riffer/rig/codingagent.rb; docs/SESSIONS.md; settings.rb. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: skills may encode reusable procedures, but this repository does not supply a learning/evolution loop that creates or revises them from environmental evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AGENTS.md instructions, tool allowlists, hooks/guardrails, settings and provider credentials constrain ordinary execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a durable governing decision for the harness. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: instruction hierarchy, request/tool guardrails, tool allowlists, settings and credentials. - Closure path: constraints affect current prompts/tool calls/configuration but do not close identity-level policy questions for subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static/configurable prompts and guardrails are execution policy mechanisms, not S5. - Evidence: prompts/agentsmd.rb; runtime/requestguardrail.rb; runtime.rb; settings.rb; README. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project maintainers and users can change instructions/configuration outside the run, but that adjacent authority is not a first-party S5 runtime loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03ba69b67f5d1b4947c68a70e1515f45faaa64f1/assessments/riffer-rig.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03ba69b67f5d1b4947c68a70e1515f45faaa64f1\">03ba69b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-21T16:49:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/markmhendrickson/ateles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ateles</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-21\">2026-10-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/markmhendrickson/ateles/tree/516edbf0bf6a96654f69b8a797fd2998aa4983cb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 516edbf0bf6a96654f69b8a797fd2998aa4983cb\">516edbf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Agent work cells perform model-guided coding, reviews, release handling, mail/calendar/content tasks in the configured personal swarm. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended task requirements and changing tool/work results, including code defects, review findings and external requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The loaded agent/skill chooses task-semantic tool actions and adapts during a headless Codex/Claude/Cursor execution; specific risky outward effects are separately gated. - Decision owner: Each dispatched, role-defined model agent within an Ateles-controlled T4 invocation; external model inference alone is not assigned supervisory authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Neotoma agentdefinition mirror/loader, signed grants, provider selector, context/system prompt, controlled subprocess, task/session records, event dispatch. - Closure path: Event/task arrives → Apis/Anthus selects role and builds scoped prompt → first-party runner launches model tool loop → result/agent-produced outputs are recorded to Neotoma, can enter follow-up workflow and approved external action. - Boundary reachability: execution/daemons/apis/apis.py::spawnharnessskill() calls shipped skillrunner.runskill(); runner constructs actual CLI commands and subprocesses for named agents, and anthus.py::spawnagent() provides another wired route. External inference is a configured dependency for shipped first-party autonomous work cell, not a separately administered employee. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The roles&#39; model-driven semantic choices and tool paths are bounded by first-party orchestration, identity and task/result recording. Removing the model leaves deterministic dispatch but no S1 problem-solving; keeping it in this shipped path realizes S1. - Evidence: api; runner; anthus; prereq; readme - Basis: structural and explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent damaging overlap between distinct S1 agent builds targeting the same repository issue and return deferred work into task execution when the collision ceases. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independent dispatched work cells can act on the same repository#issue before the first completes; uncoordinated duplicate editing/PR delivery would cause interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which in-flight build retains the issue key, hold the overlapping task with an exact deferral record, and release/re-dispatch eligible deferred work when the claim ends. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party Apis/builddelivery machinery owns atomic in-process claim and recovery; task agents execute after the coordination decision, but there is no demonstrated agent discretion in choosing the coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activebuild, claimbuild, deferralcontext, guarded Neotoma task writes, release hook, startup sweep, deferred task recovery and scoped worktree. - Closure path: A second task finds an active issue claim and becomes a durable deferredbehindbuild task; the original holder&#39;s release signals a recovery sweep, which checks fresh state and re-dispatches only qualified work. Different task agents then resume in the permitted order. - Boundary reachability: apis.py&#39;s task dispatcher and recovery paths use builddelivery and skillrunner.runskill; these are shipped Python modules, not foundation-only future requirements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The code establishes a concrete inter-S1 collision and specific attenuation with feedback; C recognizes this first-party function-specific coordination construction without pretending the model agent chooses the rules. - Evidence: delivery; api; runner - Basis: structural - Confidence: moderate-high - Caveats: The in-flight claim is explicitly process-local, not a distributed lease among independently deployed operator instances. Broader design claims are not inherited.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: ? - Function: Whole-current swarm regulation over task priorities, resource limits, external-effect commitments and corrective intervention is plausible but not fully evidenced as one organization-level S3 decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cross-domain execution costs, asynchronous task backlog, delivery collisions and approval risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A whole-system authority should choose/revise operational commitments and priorities using a global view and return changes to S1 agent work. - Decision owner: The operator controls some execution policy, while deterministic dispatch gates constrain tasks; the evidence does not yet establish a single decisive current-control owner and closed whole-system feedback path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Blast/confidence gate, role grants, configurable model tier, task queue, checkpoints, provider capacity windows and reports. - Closure path: Per-action approval/checkpoint and per-task retry are implemented, but a sourced whole-swarm performance/resource bargaining and accountable reallocation loop was not reconstructed. - Boundary reachability: No positive S3 published; the first-party task gate and current-task control paths remain plausible and prevent a bounded —. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A threshold gate and routing/scheduling are not by themselves global resource regulation; the need to inspect live, accepted priorities and operator override return paths is material. - Evidence: policy; api; arch; status - Basis: structural with unresolved semantics -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate complementary, sufficiently independent audit judgment with findings returned into a current S3 management decision is established for the shipped swarm. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untruthful task completion, inaccurate recorded evidence or mistaken routine checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct, independent access path must challenge normal S1/S3 reporting and feed corrective control. - Decision owner: Routine reviewers, workflow sign-offs, CI checks and Neotoma audit records exist; no function-specific complementary auditor/decisive feedback owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Signed identities, observation provenance, developer/tests, review gates, operator checkpoints, archive and drift records. - Closure path: Ordinary review/gate findings can block one task, but a distinct sporadic audit channel + corrective whole-system management return is not shown. - Boundary reachability: No positive state claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate reviewer role, signature or audit trail alone cannot establish S3 at the assessed agent-organization recursion. - Evidence: status; gates; arch; general - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Convert externally relevant runtime/organizational drift and contradictions into prospective policy-adjustment options for later authorization and operational adoption. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New conditions, recurring failures, tool/provider behaviours, misfit of agent operating rules against task environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Distinguish repeated environmental drift, propose changed agent policy, and present an adaptation option to governing authority for incorporation into future operating rules. - Decision owner: The first-party generalizer clusters drift and creates signed agentpolicy/revision proposals; ultimate active-policy authorization is left to the operator/approval path, with no self-promotion of live rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anthus drift harvesting, threshold/risk classification, operator-conflict detection, signed proposal write, Neotoma-backed policy reader. - Closure path: anthus harvests drift → generalizer.processsignals classifies clusters and persists policy/revision proposals → separately authorized operator must approve/adopt → live agentpolicy loader/governing runtime can change subsequent behaviour. Last integration is a configured approval/composition path, not proven autonomous promotion. - Boundary reachability: lib/daemonruntime/generalizer.py::actoncluster emits first-party rule proposals from harvested signals; execution/daemons/anthus/anthus.py invokes harvesting, and loader/gate read current policies. This is a prospective adaptation constructor path within the shipped system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete option generator and policy return surface go beyond internal task planning; C, not A, because decisions on cross-cutting rule adoption remain operator-gated. - Evidence: general; anthus; agentload; policy - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Maintain the single-operator swarm&#39;s legitimate organizational policy and identity boundary under a named ultimate human principal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cross-agent role authority, rules for what may be done autonomously, and conflicts between proposed policy drift and operator-owned governing commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Approve or reject changes to ultimate agent policies, grant scope and identity/action authority, which later runtime decisions must obey. - Decision owner: One explicitly documented founder/operator principal governs the single-operator reference. Agent-generated rule proposals cannot overwrite operator-authored policies; multi-operator ownership/quorum remains design-only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Neotoma-canonical agentpolicy, signed agent-to-principal identity and grants, operator-only blast class and runtime fail-closed policy reader, change proposals. - Closure path: Agent observes/presents proposed policy/authority revision → legitimate operator approves/adopts into canonical Neotoma policy → runtime loads resulting approved policy and applies it to subsequent agent dispatch/action gating. Human approval of individual routine PRs alone is NOT the S5 witness. - Boundary reachability: The repo ships the single-principal regime and reads effective policies in gating.loadpolicy/agent loader; generalizer explicitly defers operator-conflicting and high-layer changes to the parent. The authority binding is part of the supported P1 reference, not the hypothetical P2–P5 distributed system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent may propose; ultimate identity/policy remains with the human principal and the authorized version returns into action constraints. No autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Evidence: readme; policy; general; agentload; status - Basis: explicit and structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/907b625f88397c09eddfe65ec1374701d39e117d/assessments/at/ateles.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 907b625f88397c09eddfe65ec1374701d39e117d\">907b625 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T16:19:17Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gagans23/agent-os\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-os</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gagans23/agent-os/tree/d318787db1fce4163e470ac1c94f5a74d667303f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d318787db1fce4163e470ac1c94f5a74d667303f\">d318787 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user-submitted personal-agent tasks into model-generated operational results under persistent context and reusable skills. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, changing remembered/contextual information, matched skill procedures, provider responses, task failures and task-specific observations captured in the job trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate the substantive task response/action content from the current command plus runtime-supplied memory/skill context. - Decision owner: the configured autonomous model actor reached through the first-party provider-backed agentfn mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CommandRouter, runjob(), provider adapters, persistent memory/jobs, hooks, skill matching, traces, risk classification and configured provider transport. - Closure path: command → router/risk admission → memory and matched-skill context → provider-backed autonomous agent function → result/trace persistence → returned user result and later memory/learning availability. - Boundary reachability: a configured provider is an explicitly supported standard operating mode; CommandRouter automatically converts it to agentfn and /run calls the first-party runjob() path. No adjacent development/evaluation agent is needed to supply the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party Python code transports context and invokes the model, but the substantive response for an admitted task is selected by the autonomous model actor rather than predetermined by the router. The model service itself remains an external dependency, as with other model-backed harnesses. - Evidence: pinned agentos/providers.py, agentos/commandrouter.py, agentos/runner.py and README provider/run documentation. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: with no provider configured agent-os can remain in deterministic fallback mode; A records the supported first-party model-backed operating mode, not a claim that every configuration is agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: suppress a concrete shared-state collision among simultaneously executing swarm sub-jobs so sibling operations can proceed without corrupting or blocking one another during shared-store initialization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent workers opening the shared jobs/memory SQLite stores can race on the journal-mode/WAL transition and produce database is locked failures before useful parallel work begins. - Decisive decision or feedback right: establish the shared database schema/WAL state once before fan-out rather than letting sibling workers race to establish it independently. - Decision owner: the first-party deterministic Orchestrator implementation/developer-selected coordination policy; no autonomous agent is shown choosing or revising this conflict-response rule at runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: initshareddb(), SQLite WAL/busy-timeout behavior, ThreadPoolExecutor, per-worker JobStore/AgentMemory connections and the normal swarm execution path. - Closure path: swarm admitted → initshareddb() initializes the shared stores → parallel worker connections open against already-prepared stores → sibling sub-jobs execute and return results instead of colliding on journal initialization. - Boundary reachability: initshareddb() is wired directly and unconditionally into first-party Orchestrator.run() before ThreadPoolExecutor fan-out in the shipped /swarm path; no downstream composition is needed to expose the coordination primitive. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2 function and feedback path are concrete, but the decisive response to the inter-worker conflict is a fixed runtime policy. The model may choose sub-tasks, but it does not own this coordination discretion, so the autonomous A threshold is not met. - Evidence: pinned agentos/orchestrator.py, especially initshareddb() and the explicit implementation comment explaining the database is locked race it prevents. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this classification is not based on decomposition, parallelism or synthesis by themselves; it is based on the specific inter-S1 shared-store collision and the first-party attenuation path. C records a real S2-specific first-party path without autonomous ownership of the decisive coordination rule. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently executing swarm sub-jobs, each performing a separate user-derived operational task through runjob() and producing its own result. - Inter-S1 disturbance: sibling workers contend on common jobs/memory SQLite initialization and can race on journal-mode switching, yielding database is locked. - Attenuating coordination relation: the orchestrator initializes/closes the common stores once before worker fan-out, leaving workers to open already-prepared WAL/schema state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after pre-initialization the sibling workers can enter their normal job execution paths concurrently rather than fail at shared-store setup. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the source explicitly ties the pre-fan-out relation to a concrete concurrency collision among sibling workers; it is not credited merely because a queue, shared database or parallel executor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control function was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current job health, pending approvals, process failures, recent scores and swarm execution are observable or locally regulated, but no whole-installation resource/commitment/prioritization disturbance is shown under a qualifying S3 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor with a whole-system current view and discretionary authority over shared current resources, commitments, priorities, constraints, accountability or synergy was established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /status, /health, job statistics, risk gates, human approvals for individual privileged commands, fixed supervisor restart/backoff rules, swarm concurrency configuration and persistent job state. - Closure path: no qualifying whole-system current-control decision→subsequent-operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the available machinery observes or enforces local/static decisions. A human can approve a particular privileged action, but that is not evidence of whole-system S3 parent governance. - Evidence: pinned agentos/commandrouter.py, approvals.py, supervisor.py, orchestrator.py, jobs/health surfaces and README governance documentation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: later versions or downstream operator organizations could add S3 without changing the meaning of this frozen assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no qualifying first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established inside agent-os. - Disturbance / variety regulated: traces, commands, approval decisions, scores and daily reliability summaries are recorded/checked, but they do not establish a separate first-party challenge channel to operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party auditor with materially independent access and a findings→control return path was found. - Decision owner: not established inside the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace recording, hash-chained audit log and verify(), /audit, daily evaluation summaries, Ninja Harness invocation and persisted external scores. - Closure path: no qualifying first-party complementary audit finding is returned into current control as an independently owned S3 judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit database verifies integrity of the same recorded command/decision path; it does not independently challenge operational claims. Ninja Harness can supply a distinct evaluation judgment, but that judgment is owned by a separately scoped external system and is not imported into agent-os ownership. - Evidence: pinned agentos/audit.py, runner.py, dailyeval.py, README&#39;s explicit runtime/evaluation separation and batch-#89 boundary note. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external evaluation may still be operationally valuable and may feed adaptation proposals; the standalone VSM ownership boundary is narrower than an integrated multi-system deployment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: turn a demonstrated novel user workflow into a reusable future capability when a legitimate parent human accepts the proposed adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: successful external/user work can reveal recurring procedures not represented in the current skill library, creating future capability mismatch if the runtime cannot preserve and reuse the newly demonstrated workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the proposed reusable SKILL.md becomes an installed capability for future agent runs. - Decision owner: the human/operator parent through the explicit /approve or /reject gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace capture, success/novelty/complexity tests in proposeskill(), draft persistence, approval queue, skill-file write, SkillRegistry.reload() and later skill matching in runjob(). - Closure path: novel successful user task → first-party trace-derived skill draft → human review/approval → skill written into the live library and registry reloaded → later matching task receives the reusable procedure in its execution context. - Boundary reachability: the learning path is wired into ordinary first-party /run/approved-run completion through maybeproposeskill() and the same standard approval command surface; no custom extension or repository-development actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-os autonomously constructs the candidate adaptation, but maintainer code explicitly refuses to install it silently. The decisive capability-change right belongs to the parent human, so the published state is standalone P, not A. - Evidence: pinned agentos/skillsynth.py, agentos/commandrouter.py, agentos/skillregistry.py, approvals.py, runner.py and README learning-loop documentation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the separate weak-run ImprovementProposal path is propose-only and requires an additional apply step; it is supporting evidence of conservative adaptation design, not an autonomous S4 closure. External Ninja scores may contribute a signal in some learning paths but do not own agent-os&#39;s parent adaptation decision. - External distinction: a real user/environment task succeeds through a procedure not already covered by the installed skill set; the learned option is grounded in the task&#39;s recorded operational trace rather than generated from an internal roadmap alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the draft is created specifically as a reusable skill so later matching work can exploit the demonstrated procedure instead of rediscovering it. - Adaptation option generated: a concrete SKILL.md draft containing name, triggers, reconstructed procedure and verification instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: parent approval writes the draft to the active skills directory, reloads the registry, and subsequent runjob() calls can match/inject the new procedure into model context.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the declared installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider choice, risk rules, profiles, packs, skills and per-action approvals constrain behavior, but no evidenced identity/ultimate-policy dispute is escalated to and closed by a legitimate S5 authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying ultimate identity/policy decision path was established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured profiles/tool allowlists, pluggable risk classifier, human approval of privileged actions, provider configuration, role packs and static governance defaults. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → authority → authoritative decision → returned governing operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts/configuration and human vetoes over ordinary actions constrain S1 work but do not constitute S5 under the Profile without an identity-level issue and ultimate-policy closure. - Evidence: pinned agentos/profiles.py, risk.py, approvals.py, provider/pack configuration, command router and README governance material. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator obviously retains ordinary ownership of a self-hosted installation; the Methodology does not infer S5=P from generic configuration authority or per-task approvals.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7d01d7323bb17fab796dd0efd2045bbfa1075563/assessments/gagans-agent-os.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7d01d7323bb17fab796dd0efd2045bbfa1075563\">7d01d73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aohp-os/aohp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AOHP</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aohp-os/aohp/tree/b8ab9e98a2bd51f7fbd01a95d3b45dca078c5884\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b8ab9e98a2bd51f7fbd01a95d3b45dca078c5884\">b8ab9e9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: enact user-requested service outcomes through model-driven agents operating across application/system capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous apps/interfaces, user intent, GUI/API/CLI state, cross-app information and task-specific environmental changes during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the agent actor chooses task actions/service-composition steps under the user&#39;s intent. - Decision owner: autonomous AI agent actor hosted by the AOHP harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OS APIs/CLIs/GUI access, background execution, memory, sandboxed sensitive values and information-flow tracking. - Closure path: agent choices invoke OS/application actions, observations return through the harness and subsequent agent behaviour responds until the requested service outcome is produced. - Why agent-owned: AOHP explicitly places agents as first-class operating actors; deterministic OS mechanisms execute/constrain rather than replace the agent&#39;s task decisions. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - AOHP supports parallel background interaction and shared OS resources, but the pinned repository evidence does not identify distinct S1 units plus a specific inter-S1 interference/oscillation and a corresponding coordination feedback path. OS scheduling, sandboxing and information-flow enforcement are not promoted merely because they can constrain concurrent actors. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The OS can enforce permissions, information-flow constraints, background execution and user-consent gates, but no distinct first-party actor is evidenced with a whole-system current view plus discretionary authority over shared operational resources, commitments or priorities. - Sensitive-transfer/payment consent is an action-level authorization mechanism; it does not establish a whole-system S3 parent loop. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - Security-oriented test cases and information-flow metadata validate/enforce the product&#39;s security model, but no independent runtime audit actor/path is shown challenging ordinary S1/S3 reporting and returning corrective findings into current control. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Personalization and cross-app memory can improve task context, but the pinned boundary does not establish an externally and prospectively oriented intelligence loop that models future environmental change, develops adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - User intent and consent are important authority inputs, and AOHP&#39;s security policies constrain execution. However, the cited paths concern task/sensitive-action authorization rather than an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue reaching legitimate parent authority and returning to govern the organization as a whole. - Static/security enforcement is therefore not promoted to S5 or P. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/aohp.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
